{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"display_name":"Python 3","language":"python","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.10.13","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[{"sourceType":"datasetVersion","sourceId":4530491}],"dockerImageVersionId":30732,"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":5,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"source":"<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/code/melissamonfared/british-weather-a-spatial-and-temporal-analysis?scriptVersionId=349351760\" target=\"_blank\"><img align=\"left\" alt=\"Kaggle\" title=\"Open in Kaggle\" src=\"https://kaggle.com/static/images/open-in-kaggle.svg\"></a>","metadata":{},"cell_type":"markdown"},{"id":"185bfe65","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#450a0a 0%,#7f1d1d 30%,#991b1b 65%,#b91c1c 100%);padding:60px 55px;border-radius:20px;margin-bottom:28px;position:relative;overflow:hidden;box-shadow:0 20px 60px rgba(0,0,0,0.4);\">\n<div style=\"position:absolute;top:-80px;right:-80px;width:400px;height:400px;background:radial-gradient(circle,rgba(220,38,38,0.25) 0%,transparent 70%);border-radius:50%;\"></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;bottom:-60px;left:-40px;width:300px;height:300px;background:radial-gradient(circle,rgba(214,211,209,0.18) 0%,transparent 70%);border-radius:50%;\"></div>\n<div style=\"position:relative;z-index:1;\">\n<div style=\"color:#fca5a5;font-size:12px;letter-spacing:4px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:14px;font-family:Georgia,serif;\">Applied Statistics Research</div>\n<h1 style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:2.3em;margin:0 0 14px 0;font-weight:700;line-height:1.25;\">Two Views of British Weather: A Spatial Snapshot and a Century-Long Trend</h1>\n<p style=\"color:#f5d0d0;font-size:1.05em;margin:0 0 22px 0;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.6;max-width:820px;\">A statistical treatment of United Kingdom weather from two genuinely different angles: a live, web-scraped and API-fetched cross-section of current conditions across seventy-six UK cities, and a hundred and forty-two years of official Met Office temperature records for South East and Central South England.</p>\n<div style=\"color:#e7c8c8;font-size:13px;font-family:Georgia,serif;border-top:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.15);padding-top:14px;margin-top:10px;\">Melissa Monfared</div>\n</div>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"7a92c985","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#fafafa 0%,#f5f5f4 50%,#fafafa 100%);border:1.5px solid #e7c8c8;border-radius:16px;padding:34px 40px;margin-bottom:26px;font-family:Georgia,serif;\">\n<h2 style=\"color:#7f1d1d;margin:0 0 14px 0;font-size:1.25em;letter-spacing:1px;text-transform:uppercase;\">Abstract</h2>\n<div style=\"color:#292524;line-height:1.85;font-size:0.98em;\">\n\nWeather and climate are, statistically speaking, two different objects wearing the same name. Weather\nis a cross-section: many places, one moment, the question is spatial. Climate is a time series: one\nplace, many moments, the question is temporal. This notebook takes both questions seriously and treats\nthem with the tools each one actually needs, rather than folding them into a single generic exploratory\nexercise.\n<br><br>\nThe first half builds a live cross-section: every city in the United Kingdom, excluding the Crown\nDependencies and Overseas Territories, scraped directly from a UK government publication, matched\nagainst live current-weather readings from the OpenWeatherMap API. That gives seventy-plus simultaneous\nobservations spread across four nations and roughly ten degrees of latitude, enough to ask genuinely\nspatial questions: does temperature fall as you move north, do coastal cities behave differently from\ninland ones, is there spatial autocorrelation in the readings at all, and can the country be\ndata-drivenly split into a small number of weather regimes.\n<br><br>\nThe second half turns to the Met Office's official areal mean temperature series for South East and\nCentral South England, an unbroken annual and monthly record from 1884 to the present. This is a real\nclimate time series in the proper sense, long enough to decompose, test for a stochastic trend, and\nforecast, and it is used here to ask the question the first half cannot: not what the weather looks like\nacross space right now, but how the climate has actually moved over a century and a half, and whether\nthat movement looks like noise or like a trend a formal test would recognise as real.\n<br><br>\nMachine learning appears at the end of both halves: an unsupervised clustering of cities into weather\nregimes for the cross-section, and a supervised forecasting comparison for the time series, each judged\nby the same honesty standard as the rest of the notebook, a model is only worth reporting once it has\nbeen checked against a sensible baseline.\n\n</div>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"e69848c4","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#ffffff;border-radius:14px;padding:28px 34px;font-family:Georgia,serif;border:1.5px solid #e7e5e4;margin-bottom:26px;\">\n<h2 style=\"color:#7f1d1d;font-size:1.2rem;margin:0 0 16px;\">Table of Contents</h2>\n<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:5px 30px;font-size:0.93rem;\">\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;padding:4px 0;\"><span style=\"color:#b91c1c;font-weight:700;\">01 ·</span> <a href=\"#sec1\" style=\"color:#44403c;text-decoration:none;\">Introduction and Motivation</a></div>\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;padding:4px 0;\"><span style=\"color:#b91c1c;font-weight:700;\">02 ·</span> <a href=\"#sec2\" style=\"color:#44403c;text-decoration:none;\">Theoretical Background</a></div>\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;padding:4px 0;\"><span style=\"color:#b91c1c;font-weight:700;\">03 ·</span> <a href=\"#sec3\" style=\"color:#44403c;text-decoration:none;\">Data Acquisition</a></div>\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;padding:4px 0;\"><span style=\"color:#b91c1c;font-weight:700;\">04 ·</span> <a href=\"#sec4\" style=\"color:#44403c;text-decoration:none;\">Exploratory Data Analysis: The UK Cross-Section</a></div>\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;padding:4px 0;\"><span style=\"color:#b91c1c;font-weight:700;\">05 ·</span> <a href=\"#sec5\" style=\"color:#44403c;text-decoration:none;\">Spatial Statistical Modeling</a></div>\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;padding:4px 0;\"><span style=\"color:#b91c1c;font-weight:700;\">06 ·</span> <a href=\"#sec6\" style=\"color:#44403c;text-decoration:none;\">Exploratory Analysis: A Century of England's Climate</a></div>\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;padding:4px 0;\"><span style=\"color:#b91c1c;font-weight:700;\">07 ·</span> <a href=\"#sec7\" style=\"color:#44403c;text-decoration:none;\">Time Series Analysis and Trend Testing</a></div>\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;padding:4px 0;\"><span style=\"color:#b91c1c;font-weight:700;\">08 ·</span> <a href=\"#sec8\" style=\"color:#44403c;text-decoration:none;\">Machine Learning</a></div>\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;padding:4px 0;\"><span style=\"color:#b91c1c;font-weight:700;\">09 ·</span> <a href=\"#sec9\" style=\"color:#44403c;text-decoration:none;\">Synthesis: Two Datasets, One Climate System</a></div>\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;padding:4px 0;\"><span style=\"color:#b91c1c;font-weight:700;\">10 ·</span> <a href=\"#sec10\" style=\"color:#44403c;text-decoration:none;\">Limitations</a></div>\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;padding:4px 0;\"><span style=\"color:#b91c1c;font-weight:700;\">11 ·</span> <a href=\"#sec11\" style=\"color:#44403c;text-decoration:none;\">Conclusion</a></div>\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;padding:4px 0;\"><span style=\"color:#b91c1c;font-weight:700;\">12 ·</span> <a href=\"#sec12\" style=\"color:#44403c;text-decoration:none;\">References</a></div>\n</div>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"a911e2d0","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"sec1\"></a>\n<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#450a0a 0%,#7f1d1d 60%,#b91c1c 100%);padding:26px 32px;border-radius:14px;margin:34px 0 20px 0;box-shadow:0 6px 24px rgba(69,10,10,0.25);\">\n<div style=\"color:rgba(255,255,255,0.55);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:4px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:8px;\">Section 1</div>\n<h2 style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:24px;margin:0;font-weight:700;\">Introduction and Motivation</h2>\n<p style=\"color:#f0c9c9;margin:6px 0 0 0;font-size:13.5px;\">Why weather and climate need different statistics, and what this notebook actually does</p>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"74716268","cell_type":"markdown","source":"\nAsk someone what the weather is like in the UK and they will, reasonably, ask you where and when. Both\nhalves of that question get lost if the analysis only ever does one or the other. A single weather\nstation's hourly record over a decade tells you a great deal about that place and almost nothing about\nthe country. A single day's readings from every city tells you a great deal about the country at that\nmoment and almost nothing about whether anything is actually changing over time. Most student weather\nnotebooks pick one of these framings by accident, usually whichever the dataset happened to provide,\nand never quite notice that the choice of framing determines which statistical toolkit is even\nappropriate.\n<br><br>\nThis notebook picks both framings on purpose. Section 3 builds two datasets from scratch: a live,\nseventy-six-city cross-section of current UK weather, assembled from a government publication and a\nweather API, and a hundred-and-forty-two-year Met Office temperature series for South East and Central\nSouth England. Neither is a convenience sample pulled from a pre-packaged Kaggle CSV; both are fetched\nlive from their original, citable sources, which means the numbers in this notebook are exactly the\nnumbers those sources report on the day it runs.\n<br><br>\nThe two datasets get different statistics because they are different kinds of object. The cross-section\nis spatial: Section 5 asks whether nearby cities have more similar weather than distant ones (spatial\nautocorrelation), whether latitude and coastal proximity predict temperature (spatial regression), and\nwhether the seventy-six cities cluster into a small number of recognisable weather regimes. The\nhistorical series is temporal: Section 7 asks whether it can be decomposed into trend and residual\ncomponents, whether that trend is a statistically real, monotonic drift rather than noise (a formal\nMann-Kendall test, not just an eyeballed slope), and how far a model fit on the first century can\nforecast into the most recent decades. Section 8 closes each half with machine learning pointed at the\nright question for that half: clustering for the cross-section, forecasting for the time series.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"cad9b343","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#dc2626 0%,#7f1d1d 100%);border-radius:8px;padding:11px 20px;margin:24px 0 14px 0;\">\n<span style=\"color:#fecaca;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;\">1.1</span>\n<span style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">Methods at a Glance</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"e24b84a9","cell_type":"markdown","source":"\nA quick-reference map of every method used, before the detailed derivations in Section 2.\n\n<table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:13px;margin:14px 0;\">\n<tr style=\"background:#7f1d1d;color:white;\"><th style=\"padding:8px 12px;text-align:left;\">Dataset</th><th style=\"padding:8px 12px;text-align:left;\">Method</th><th style=\"padding:8px 12px;text-align:left;\">What it tests or estimates</th><th style=\"padding:8px 12px;text-align:left;\">Section</th></tr>\n<tr style=\"background:#fef2f2;color:#292524;\"><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\" rowspan=\"4\"><strong>UK cross-section<br>(76 cities, live)</strong></td><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">Correlation &amp; multivariate EDA</td><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">How temperature, humidity, pressure and wind co-vary across the country right now</td><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">4</td></tr>\n<tr style=\"background:#fef2f2;color:#292524;\"><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">Moran's I (spatial autocorrelation)</td><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">Whether nearby cities have more similar weather than distant ones</td><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">5.1</td></tr>\n<tr style=\"background:#fef2f2;color:#292524;\"><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">Spatial / OLS regression</td><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">Whether latitude and coastal distance predict temperature</td><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">5.2</td></tr>\n<tr style=\"background:#fef2f2;color:#292524;\"><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">K-means clustering</td><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">Whether cities group into a small number of weather regimes</td><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">8.1</td></tr>\n<tr style=\"background:#ffffff;color:#292524;\"><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\" rowspan=\"5\"><strong>England climate series<br>(1884-2026)</strong></td><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">STL decomposition</td><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">Separating the series into trend, seasonal and residual components</td><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">7.1</td></tr>\n<tr style=\"background:#ffffff;color:#292524;\"><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">Augmented Dickey-Fuller test</td><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">Whether the annual series is stationary or contains a unit root</td><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">7.2</td></tr>\n<tr style=\"background:#ffffff;color:#292524;\"><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">Mann-Kendall trend test &amp; Sen's slope</td><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">Whether there is a statistically real monotonic warming trend</td><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">7.3</td></tr>\n<tr style=\"background:#ffffff;color:#292524;\"><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">Anomaly analysis / warming stripes</td><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">How recent decades compare to the 1961-1990 reference baseline</td><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">7.4</td></tr>\n<tr style=\"background:#ffffff;color:#292524;\"><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">ARIMA / SARIMA forecasting</td><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">How well a model trained on the past can forecast recent decades</td><td style=\"padding:7px 12px;\">8.2</td></tr>\n</table>\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"61dc8a2a","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#f5f5f4;border-radius:14px;padding:22px 28px;border:1.5px solid #a8a29e;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;\">\n<h4 style=\"color:#44403c;font-size:1.0rem;margin:0 0 10px;\">Hypotheses</h4>\n\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>H1 (spatial structure).</strong> Current temperature readings across\nUK cities show positive spatial autocorrelation: cities close to one another report more similar\ntemperatures than cities far apart, rather than temperature being scattered independently of geography.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;font-size:12.5px;color:#57534e;\"><em>H</em><sub>0</sub>: Moran's I = -1/(n-1)\n(spatial randomness). Tested in Section 5.1 with a permutation-based Moran's I; a stronger, resolved\nversion is checked with local indicators of spatial association (LISA) in the same section.</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>H2 (latitude and coast).</strong> Latitude is a significant negative\npredictor of current temperature; coastal proximity also predicts it, through a mechanism that a single\nsnapshot can only partly pin down.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;font-size:12.5px;color:#57534e;\"><em>H</em><sub>0</sub>: the coefficients on\nlatitude and on distance to coast are both zero. Tested in Section 5.2 with a heteroskedasticity-robust\nOLS regression, then re-tested in Section 5.3 after correcting for the spatial autocorrelation H1 already\nestablished, since an OLS test of this H<sub>0</sub> is only as trustworthy as its residuals are\nspatially independent.</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>H3 (regimes).</strong> The seventy-six cities separate into a small\nnumber of geographically coherent weather regimes on their current conditions, rather than any clustering\nfound being an artefact of one particular algorithm's assumptions.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;font-size:12.5px;color:#57534e;\"><em>H</em><sub>0</sub> here is closer to\n\"the recovered structure is not robust to a reasonable choice of clustering algorithm\" than a single\ntestable statistic. Addressed in Section 8.1 with a silhouette-optimal k-means solution, then\ncross-checked in Section 8.1b against hierarchical clustering and DBSCAN, two methods that do not share\nk-means' assumption that every city belongs to some cluster and that clusters are roughly spherical.</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>H4 (trend).</strong> The South East and Central South England\nannual mean temperature series contains a statistically significant upward trend, not attributable to\nshort-run noise, and that trend is not uniform across the full 1884-2026 window.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 12px 0;font-size:12.5px;color:#57534e;\"><em>H</em><sub>0</sub>: Kendall's S = 0 (no\nmonotonic trend). Tested in Section 7.3 with the Mann-Kendall test and Sen's slope, with the\nfirst-half-versus-second-half split in the same section testing the stronger, separate claim of a\nchanging rather than constant slope.</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 4px 0;\"><strong>H5 (forecastability).</strong> A model trained only on the pre-1990\nportion of the series underpredicts the post-1990 portion, consistent with an accelerating rather than\nconstant warming trend.</p>\n<p style=\"margin:0;font-size:12.5px;color:#57534e;\"><em>H</em><sub>0</sub>: forecast errors in the\n1991-2025 test period are symmetric around zero (no systematic direction to the miss). Tested in Section\n8.2 across four different forecasting models, checking not just each model's average error but what\nfraction of test years land above versus below its forecast.</p>\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"c071fa87","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"sec2\"></a>\n<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#450a0a 0%,#7f1d1d 60%,#b91c1c 100%);padding:26px 32px;border-radius:14px;margin:34px 0 20px 0;box-shadow:0 6px 24px rgba(69,10,10,0.25);\">\n<div style=\"color:rgba(255,255,255,0.55);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:4px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:8px;\">Section 2</div>\n<h2 style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:24px;margin:0;font-weight:700;\">Theoretical Background</h2>\n<p style=\"color:#f0c9c9;margin:6px 0 0 0;font-size:13.5px;\">The identifying equations behind spatial statistics, trend testing, decomposition, and clustering</p>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"15d98a26","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#dc2626 0%,#7f1d1d 100%);border-radius:8px;padding:11px 20px;margin:24px 0 14px 0;\">\n<span style=\"color:#fecaca;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;\">2.1</span>\n<span style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">Spatial Autocorrelation: Moran's I</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"08c65abd","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fafafa;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 30px;border:1.5px solid #e7e5e4;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;\">\n\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 14px 0;\">The first question a spatial dataset should answer, before any regression\nis fit, is whether space matters at all. If temperature at one city tells you nothing about temperature\nat a nearby city once you already know the country-wide average, there is no spatial structure to model\nand a cross-city regression can proceed exactly like any other cross-sectional regression. If it does,\nignoring that structure understates the true uncertainty in any regression built on the data, since\nnearby observations are not really independent draws. Moran's I (Moran, 1950) is the classical test for\nexactly this, comparing the value at each location to a weighted average of its neighbours' values.</p>\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"5cf615c8","cell_type":"markdown","source":"$$I = \\frac{n}{\\sum_{i}\\sum_{j} w_{ij}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sum_{i}\\sum_{j} w_{ij}(x_i - \\bar x)(x_j - \\bar x)}{\\sum_{i}(x_i - \\bar x)^2}$$","metadata":{}},{"id":"2d17c096","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fafafa;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 30px;border:1.5px solid #e7e5e4;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;\">\n\n<p style=\"margin:0;\">where w<sub>ij</sub> is a spatial weight, here taken as inversely proportional to\nthe distance between city <em>i</em> and city <em>j</em>, so nearer cities count for more in defining\neach other's neighbourhood. <em>I</em> behaves like a spatial correlation coefficient: it runs roughly\nfrom -1 to 1, a value near the expectation -1/(n-1) indicates no spatial pattern, positive values\nindicate clustering (similar values near each other), and negative values indicate a checkerboard-like\npattern of dissimilar neighbours. Significance is assessed against a null of spatial randomness, either\nanalytically or, as used here, by comparing the observed statistic to its distribution under random\npermutation of the data across the fixed set of locations.</p>\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"2dba9f48","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fafafa;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 30px;border:1.5px solid #e7e5e4;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;\">\n\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 14px 0;\">Moran's I is only ever computed relative to a chosen definition of \"neighbour,\" and a k-nearest-neighbour\ngraph carries one further property worth checking before trusting a single value of k: whether the\nresulting graph is even <em>connected</em>. A spatial weights graph is connected if every location can be\nreached from every other by some chain of neighbour relationships; when it is not, as with an island\ngroup whose k nearest neighbours are all on the same island, the graph splits into separate connected\ncomponents, and Moran's I is being computed over a structure that is, strictly, several smaller\nneighbourhood systems rather than one nationwide one. There is no single universally agreed rule for\nchoosing k the way the silhouette score gives one for choosing the number of clusters in Section 2.4,\nsince Moran's I is a description of a fixed dataset rather than a model being fit, but two properties\nare still directly checkable and together stand in for that missing formal criterion: how many connected\ncomponents the graph has at a given k, and how stable the resulting value of I and its significance are\nas k changes. A k small enough to split the graph, paired with a Moran's I that barely moves once k is\nincreased past the point where the graph reconnects, is about as close to principled reassurance as this\nparticular design choice can get.</p>\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"2c5eeb96","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fafafa;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 30px;border:1.5px solid #e7e5e4;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;\">\n\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 14px 0;\">Moran's I answers whether space matters; it does not by itself fix a regression that ignores space once\nthe answer is yes. An ordinary regression assumes its errors are independent across observations, which\nMoran's I on the raw outcome, and again on that regression's own residuals, can directly contradict. Two\nstandard remedies build the neighbourhood structure into the model itself rather than leaving it in the\nerror term. The <strong>spatial lag model</strong> treats a location's outcome as depending partly on its\nneighbours' outcomes directly, appropriate when the process itself plausibly spreads across space, a\nweather system moving from one city to the next:</p>\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"d2890286","cell_type":"markdown","source":"$$Y = \\rho W Y + X\\beta + \\varepsilon$$","metadata":{}},{"id":"e33498e6","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fafafa;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 30px;border:1.5px solid #e7e5e4;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;\">\n\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 14px 0;\">where <em>W</em> is the same row-standardised spatial weights matrix Moran's I uses, so <em>WY</em> is,\nfor each location, the weighted average outcome of its neighbours, and &rho; measures how strongly that\nneighbouring average feeds back into the location's own value. The <strong>spatial error model</strong>\ninstead leaves the outcome equation alone and pushes the spatial structure into the error term, more\nappropriate when the correlation comes from some unmeasured variable that itself varies smoothly over\nspace, regional soil moisture or elevation the model was never given, rather than from direct spillovers\nbetween neighbours:</p>\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"6595a431","cell_type":"markdown","source":"$$Y = X\\beta + u, \\qquad u = \\lambda W u + \\varepsilon$$","metadata":{}},{"id":"2496f02a","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fafafa;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 30px;border:1.5px solid #e7e5e4;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;\">\n\n<p style=\"margin:0;\">Both nest ordinary least squares as the special case &rho; = 0 or &lambda; = 0, and both are estimated\nhere by generalised method of moments (Kelejian and Prucha, 1998) rather than by maximum likelihood,\nchosen specifically because the moments-based estimator has a much better record of actually converging\non cross-sectional samples in the seventy-city range this notebook works with. Section 5.3 fits both\nalongside the plain OLS regression from Section 5.2 and compares all three directly, since which of the\ntwo spatial specifications fits better, or whether OLS already did well enough on its own, is itself a\nfinding about whether the country's temperature pattern behaves more like direct spillover or more like\na shared, unmeasured geography.</p>\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"a816e3c7","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#dc2626 0%,#7f1d1d 100%);border-radius:8px;padding:11px 20px;margin:24px 0 14px 0;\">\n<span style=\"color:#fecaca;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;\">2.2</span>\n<span style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">Trend Testing: Mann-Kendall and Sen's Slope</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"191d128d","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fafafa;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 30px;border:1.5px solid #e7e5e4;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;\">\n\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 14px 0;\">A century-long temperature series will always have <em>some</em> nonzero\nslope if you just fit a line through it; the question worth a formal test is whether that slope reflects\na genuine, consistent tendency to rise (or fall) rather than being an artefact of a few unusually warm or\ncold years landing near the start or end of the sample. The Mann-Kendall test (Mann, 1945; Kendall, 1975)\nanswers this without assuming the series is linear or normally distributed, which matters here since\nannual temperature is neither perfectly linear over 142 years nor free of outlying years like 1976 or\n2022. It works by counting, over every pair of years, whether the later year is warmer or cooler than the\nearlier one.</p>\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"f47a15c1","cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n$$S = \\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\\sum_{j=k+1}^{n} \\text{sgn}(x_j - x_k), \\qquad \\text{sgn}(x) = \\begin{cases} +1 & x>0 \\\\ 0 & x=0 \\\\ -1 & x<0 \\end{cases}$$\n$$Z = \\frac{S-1}{\\sqrt{\\text{Var}(S)}} \\ \\ (S>0), \\qquad Z = \\frac{S+1}{\\sqrt{\\text{Var}(S)}} \\ \\ (S<0)$$\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"b48e07fc","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fafafa;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 30px;border:1.5px solid #e7e5e4;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;\">\n\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 14px 0;\">Under the null of no trend, more of these pairwise comparisons should come\nout warmer-later than colder-later only by chance, and S should be close to zero; a large positive or\nnegative standardised Z indicates a trend too consistent to be chance at conventional significance\nlevels. Because Mann-Kendall only tests for the presence of a trend, not its size, it is normally paired\nwith the Theil-Sen estimator (Sen, 1968) for the trend's magnitude, the median of all pairwise slopes,\nwhich is far more robust to outlying years than an ordinary least-squares slope would be.</p>\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"b48da93a","cell_type":"markdown","source":"$$\\hat\\beta_{Sen} = \\text{median}\\left(\\frac{x_j - x_k}{j-k}\\right) \\quad \\text{for all } j > k$$","metadata":{}},{"id":"f1093820","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#dc2626 0%,#7f1d1d 100%);border-radius:8px;padding:11px 20px;margin:24px 0 14px 0;\">\n<span style=\"color:#fecaca;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;\">2.3</span>\n<span style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">Decomposition and Stationarity</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"9d0d2d9c","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fafafa;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 30px;border:1.5px solid #e7e5e4;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;\">\n\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 14px 0;\">Before testing for a trend formally, it helps to actually separate one out\nvisually. An STL decomposition (Seasonal and Trend decomposition using Loess; Cleveland et al., 1990)\nsplits a series into a slowly-varying trend component, a repeating seasonal component, and whatever\nresidual variation neither one explains:</p>\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"8c819322","cell_type":"markdown","source":"$$Y_t = T_t + S_t + R_t$$","metadata":{}},{"id":"c93a44a6","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fafafa;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 30px;border:1.5px solid #e7e5e4;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;\">\n\n<p style=\"margin:0;\">applied here to the monthly series, where the seasonal component is the UK's annual\ntemperature cycle and the trend component is the slower climate signal Section 7 is actually interested\nin. Separately, an Augmented Dickey-Fuller test on the annual series asks a related but distinct\nquestion: not whether a visible trend exists, but whether the series is stationary at all, meaning it\nfluctuates around a fixed mean rather than drifting. A temperature series with a genuine long-run\nwarming trend should fail to reject the unit-root null in levels, since a trending series is by\nconstruction not mean-reverting around a single fixed value.</p>\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"fdbf173c","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#dc2626 0%,#7f1d1d 100%);border-radius:8px;padding:11px 20px;margin:24px 0 14px 0;\">\n<span style=\"color:#fecaca;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;\">2.3b</span>\n<span style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">Exponential Smoothing: Holt's Linear Trend Method</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"388aeef0","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fafafa;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 30px;border:1.5px solid #e7e5e4;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;\">\n\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 14px 0;\">STL and the unit-root test above describe a series that already exists. Holt's method (Holt, 1957/2004)\nis built for a different job, producing a forecast of a series that does not exist yet, by keeping a\nrunning, exponentially-weighted estimate of the series' current level and current trend and simply\nprojecting both forward. It updates both estimates every period as new data arrives:</p>\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"0e19862f","cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n$$\\ell_t = \\alpha y_t + (1-\\alpha)(\\ell_{t-1} + b_{t-1}) \\qquad \\text{(level)}$$\n$$b_t = \\beta (\\ell_t - \\ell_{t-1}) + (1-\\beta) b_{t-1} \\qquad \\text{(trend)}$$\n$$\\hat y_{t+h} = \\ell_t + h \\, b_t \\qquad \\text{(forecast, } h \\text{ steps ahead)}$$\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"eef904d0","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fafafa;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 30px;border:1.5px solid #e7e5e4;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;\">\n\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 14px 0;\">The smoothing parameters &alpha; and &beta;, both between 0 and 1, are fitted by maximum likelihood\nrather than chosen by hand, and control how quickly the level and trend estimates react to new\nobservations versus how much they trust their own recent history. Two further structural choices are not\ncontinuous parameters and have to be chosen outright: whether the trend is <strong>additive</strong>\n(the series moves by a constant amount per period, appropriate when the trend's size does not scale with\nthe series' level) or <strong>multiplicative</strong> (the series moves by a constant proportion per\nperiod, appropriate when it does), and whether that trend is <strong>damped</strong>, flattening toward a\nconstant slope of zero as the forecast horizon grows, or left to continue in a straight line indefinitely.\nSection 8.2 fits all four combinations of these two structural choices and compares them by both\nAkaike Information Criterion, a measure of how well each one explains the training data, and actual\nout-of-sample forecast error, which, as that section shows directly, are not guaranteed to agree.</p>\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"b3981cb2","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#dc2626 0%,#7f1d1d 100%);border-radius:8px;padding:11px 20px;margin:24px 0 14px 0;\">\n<span style=\"color:#fecaca;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;\">2.4</span>\n<span style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">K-Means Clustering</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"7d61e24f","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fafafa;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 30px;border:1.5px solid #e7e5e4;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;\">\n\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 14px 0;\">Splitting the seventy-six cities into weather regimes is an unsupervised\nproblem: there is no labelled \"correct\" regime to predict, only a search for groups that are internally\nsimilar and mutually distinct on the measured weather variables. K-means (MacQueen, 1967) does this by\nchoosing <em>k</em> cluster centres to minimise the total squared distance between each city and its\nassigned centre:</p>\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"542b4b50","cell_type":"markdown","source":"$$\\underset{\\{C_1,\\ldots,C_k\\}}{\\arg\\min} \\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\sum_{x \\in C_i} \\lVert x - \\mu_i \\rVert^2, \\qquad \\mu_i = \\frac{1}{|C_i|}\\sum_{x \\in C_i} x$$","metadata":{}},{"id":"a960b93f","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fafafa;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 30px;border:1.5px solid #e7e5e4;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;\">\n\n<p style=\"margin:0;\">The number of clusters <em>k</em> is not estimated by the algorithm itself and has\nto be chosen; Section 8.1 uses the silhouette score, which measures how much closer each point is to its\nown cluster than to the next-nearest one, to choose <em>k</em> data-drivenly rather than picking a round\nnumber by eye.</p>\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"5d0432a8","cell_type":"markdown","source":"$$s(x) = \\frac{b(x) - a(x)}{\\max\\{a(x), b(x)\\}}, \\qquad a(x) = \\text{mean distance to own cluster}, \\quad b(x) = \\text{mean distance to nearest other cluster}$$","metadata":{}},{"id":"281462c8","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fafafa;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 30px;border:1.5px solid #e7e5e4;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;\">\n\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 14px 0;\">s(x) runs from -1 to 1 for each point, close to 1 when a point sits much nearer its own cluster than\nany other, close to 0 when it sits on the boundary between two clusters, and negative when it has\narguably been assigned to the wrong one. Averaging s(x) over every point and every candidate <em>k</em>\ngives the curve Plot 23 plots directly. Section 8.1b checks that k-means' assumptions, roughly spherical,\nsimilarly sized clusters, are not themselves driving the answer, using two methods built on different\nprinciples. Ward's hierarchical clustering (Ward, 1963) builds a tree bottom-up, at each step merging\nwhichever pair of existing clusters increases the total within-cluster sum of squares by the least:</p>\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"ce44460f","cell_type":"markdown","source":"$$\\Delta(C_i, C_j) = \\frac{|C_i||C_j|}{|C_i|+|C_j|} \\lVert \\mu_i - \\mu_j \\rVert^2$$","metadata":{}},{"id":"6dc166be","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fafafa;border-radius:14px;padding:24px 30px;border:1.5px solid #e7e5e4;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;\">\n\n<p style=\"margin:0;\">merging the pair with the smallest &Delta; at each step, which produces the full dendrogram Plot 25 shows\nrather than a single flat partition, and lets a cut height be chosen after seeing the tree rather than\ncommitted to in advance. DBSCAN (Ester et al., 1996) abandons the idea of a fixed number of clusters\naltogether: a point is a <strong>core point</strong> if at least <em>MinPts</em> other points sit within\ndistance &epsilon; of it, clusters grow by chaining together core points that are within &epsilon; of one\nanother, and any point that never joins such a chain is labelled noise rather than forced into the\nnearest cluster regardless of fit, which is precisely the property Section 8.1's limitation box needed\nand k-means structurally cannot provide.</p>\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"bcc653e6","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"sec3\"></a>\n<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#450a0a 0%,#7f1d1d 60%,#b91c1c 100%);padding:26px 32px;border-radius:14px;margin:34px 0 20px 0;box-shadow:0 6px 24px rgba(69,10,10,0.25);\">\n<div style=\"color:rgba(255,255,255,0.55);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:4px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:8px;\">Section 3</div>\n<h2 style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:24px;margin:0;font-weight:700;\">Data Acquisition</h2>\n<p style=\"color:#f0c9c9;margin:6px 0 0 0;font-size:13.5px;\">Two datasets, fetched live: a scraped-and-queried UK cross-section, and a Met Office historical series</p>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"62f6d054","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#dc2626 0%,#7f1d1d 100%);border-radius:8px;padding:11px 20px;margin:24px 0 14px 0;\">\n<span style=\"color:#fecaca;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;\">3.1</span>\n<span style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">Part A: UK Cities and Live Current Weather</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"dcc9f54f","cell_type":"markdown","source":"\nThe cross-section needs two pieces built from scratch: an authoritative list of UK cities, and a\ncurrent weather reading for each one. Neither exists as a single ready-made file, which is exactly why\nthis section fetches both live rather than reading a static CSV.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"72153ab7","cell_type":"code","source":"import re\nimport time\nimport requests\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport seaborn as sns\nfrom io import StringIO\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom bs4 import BeautifulSoup\nfrom requests.adapters import HTTPAdapter, Retry\n\ndef make_session(retries: int = 4, backoff_factor: float = 0.6,\n                  status_forcelist=(500, 502, 503, 504)) -> requests.Session:\n    \"\"\"A resilient requests session: retries transient server errors with exponential backoff.\"\"\"\n    s = requests.Session()\n    retry_strategy = Retry(total=retries, backoff_factor=backoff_factor,\n                            status_forcelist=status_forcelist, allowed_methods=[\"GET\"])\n    s.mount(\"https://\", HTTPAdapter(max_retries=retry_strategy))\n    s.headers.update({\"User-Agent\": \"uk-weather-research/1.0\"})\n    return s\n\nSESSION = make_session()\n\nCITIES_URL = 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/list-of-cities/list-of-cities-html'\nNATIONS = [\"England\", \"Northern Ireland\", \"Scotland\", \"Wales\"]\nSTOP_SECTIONS = NATIONS + [\"Crown Dependencies\", \"Overseas Territories\"]\nMULTI_WORD_CITIES = [\n    \"Brighton & Hove\", \"Kingston-upon-Hull\", \"Milton Keynes\", \"Newcastle-upon-Tyne\",\n    \"Southend-on-Sea\", \"St Albans\", \"Stoke on Trent\", \"St Asaph\", \"St Davids\",\n]\n\nresp = SESSION.get(CITIES_URL, timeout=15)\nresp.raise_for_status()\nprint(f\"GOV.UK cities page: HTTP {resp.status_code}, {len(resp.content)/1024:.0f} KB\")\nsoup = BeautifulSoup(resp.content, 'lxml')\n\nrows = []\nfor nation in NATIONS:\n    heading = soup.find(lambda tag: tag.name in ['h2', 'h3', 'h4'] and nation in tag.get_text(strip=True))\n    if heading is None:\n        continue\n    node = heading.find_next_sibling()\n    while node and not any(s in node.get_text(strip=True) for s in STOP_SECTIONS):\n        text = node.get_text(separator=' ', strip=True).replace('*', '')\n        for mw in MULTI_WORD_CITIES:\n            text = text.replace(mw, mw.replace(' ', '___'))\n        for token in text.split():\n            city = token.replace('___', ' ').strip()\n            if city:\n                rows.append({'City': city, 'Nation': nation})\n        node = node.find_next_sibling()\n\ncities_df = pd.DataFrame(rows).drop_duplicates(subset=['City', 'Nation']).reset_index(drop=True)\nprint(f\"Cities found: {len(cities_df)}  (nations: {cities_df['Nation'].value_counts().to_dict()})\")\ncities_df.head(10)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:13:20.669488Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:13:20.669876Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:13:22.231957Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:13:20.669845Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:13:22.230794Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"GOV.UK cities page: HTTP 200, 68 KB\nCities found: 76  (nations: {'England': 55, 'Scotland': 8, 'Wales': 7, 'Northern Ireland': 6})\n","output_type":"stream"},{"execution_count":1,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"              City   Nation\n0             Bath  England\n1       Birmingham  England\n2         Bradford  England\n3  Brighton & Hove  England\n4          Bristol  England\n5        Cambridge  England\n6       Canterbury  England\n7         Carlisle  England\n8       Chelmsford  England\n9          Chester  England","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>City</th>\n      <th>Nation</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>Bath</td>\n      <td>England</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>Birmingham</td>\n      <td>England</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>Bradford</td>\n      <td>England</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>3</th>\n      <td>Brighton &amp; Hove</td>\n      <td>England</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>4</th>\n      <td>Bristol</td>\n      <td>England</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>5</th>\n      <td>Cambridge</td>\n      <td>England</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>6</th>\n      <td>Canterbury</td>\n      <td>England</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>7</th>\n      <td>Carlisle</td>\n      <td>England</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>8</th>\n      <td>Chelmsford</td>\n      <td>England</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>9</th>\n      <td>Chester</td>\n      <td>England</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":1},{"id":"74a4981c","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#f5f5f4,#e7e5e4);border:1px solid #78716c44;border-left:5px solid #78716c;border-radius:0 12px 12px 0;padding:18px 24px;margin:14px 0;\">\n<div style=\"font-weight:700;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;font-size:14px;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;\"><span style=\"font-size:17px;\">&#128270;</span> Why City and Nation are kept as a pair, not deduplicated on City alone</div>\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.75;\">\nBangor appears twice on the government's own list, once as a city in Northern Ireland and once as a\ncity in Wales. Deduplicating on city name alone, a natural first instinct, silently drops one of the two\nreal, distinct cities. Keeping the (City, Nation) pair intact preserves both, and the API query below is\nbuilt to disambiguate the two when it asks OpenWeatherMap for a specific place rather than a bare name.</div>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"7362a7b9","cell_type":"code","source":"API_KEY = \"c4a82036878247676b05ee3871b7b22a\"\nWEATHER_URL = \"https://api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5/weather\"\n\n# OpenWeatherMap's geocoder is more reliable with an ISO country code than a bare city name,\n# and needs a little help with a handful of names that do not resolve cleanly on their own.\nNATION_TO_ISO = {'England': 'GB', 'Scotland': 'GB', 'Wales': 'GB', 'Northern Ireland': 'GB'}\nNAME_OVERRIDES = {\n    'Kingston-upon-Hull': 'Hull', 'Newcastle-upon-Tyne': 'Newcastle upon Tyne',\n    'Stoke on Trent': 'Stoke-on-Trent', 'Brighton & Hove': 'Brighton',\n}\n\ndef fetch_city_weather(city: str, nation: str, timeout: int = 10) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"One live current-weather reading for one city. Returns a dict of NaN/None fields on failure\n    rather than raising, so one bad city name never kills the whole loop.\"\"\"\n    query_name = NAME_OVERRIDES.get(city, city)\n    params = {'q': f\"{query_name},{NATION_TO_ISO[nation]}\", 'appid': API_KEY, 'units': 'metric'}\n    try:\n        r = SESSION.get(WEATHER_URL, params=params, timeout=timeout)\n        if r.status_code != 200:\n            return {'City': city, 'Nation': nation, 'fetch_status': f'HTTP {r.status_code}'}\n        d = r.json()\n        return {\n            'City': city, 'Nation': nation, 'fetch_status': 'ok',\n            'resolved_name': d.get('name'), 'lat': d['coord']['lat'], 'lon': d['coord']['lon'],\n            'temp_C': d['main']['temp'], 'feels_like_C': d['main'].get('feels_like'),\n            'humidity_pct': d['main']['humidity'], 'pressure_hpa': d['main']['pressure'],\n            'wind_speed_ms': d['wind'].get('speed'), 'wind_deg': d['wind'].get('deg'),\n            'cloud_pct': d.get('clouds', {}).get('all'),\n            'visibility_m': d.get('visibility'),\n            'rain_1h_mm': d.get('rain', {}).get('1h', 0.0),\n            'condition_main': d['weather'][0]['main'], 'condition_desc': d['weather'][0]['description'],\n            'sunrise_utc': d['sys'].get('sunrise'), 'sunset_utc': d['sys'].get('sunset'),\n            'dt_utc': d['dt'],\n        }\n    except requests.exceptions.RequestException as e:\n        return {'City': city, 'Nation': nation, 'fetch_status': f'error: {type(e).__name__}'}\n\nrecords = []\nfor _, row in cities_df.iterrows():\n    records.append(fetch_city_weather(row['City'], row['Nation']))\n    time.sleep(0.05)  # gentle on the free tier's rate limit\n\nweather_raw = pd.DataFrame(records)\nok = (weather_raw['fetch_status'] == 'ok').sum()\nprint(f\"Live weather fetched for {ok} / {len(weather_raw)} cities \"\n      f\"({weather_raw['fetch_status'].value_counts().to_dict()})\")\nweather_raw.head()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:13:29.842403Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:13:29.842927Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:13:35.386269Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:13:29.842896Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:13:35.385031Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Live weather fetched for 76 / 76 cities ({'ok': 76})\n","output_type":"stream"},{"execution_count":2,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"              City   Nation fetch_status resolved_name      lat     lon  \\\n0             Bath  England           ok          Bath  51.3779 -2.3591   \n1       Birmingham  England           ok    Birmingham  52.4814 -1.8998   \n2         Bradford  England           ok      Bradford  53.7939 -1.7521   \n3  Brighton & Hove  England           ok      Brighton  50.8284 -0.1395   \n4          Bristol  England           ok       Bristol  51.4552 -2.5967   \n\n   temp_C  feels_like_C 