r:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">Part B: A Century and a Half of England's Climate</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"8af42c78","cell_type":"markdown","source":"\nThe second dataset is the Met Office's own areal series for South East and Central South England, one\nof the regional series that feed into the UK's official HadUK-Grid climate product. It is published as\na plain fixed-width text file, updated monthly, with one row per year and one column per month plus\nfour seasons and an annual mean.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"436eb921","cell_type":"code","source":"CLIMATE_URL = 'https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/pub/data/weather/uk/climate/datasets/Tmean/date/England_SE_and_Central_S.txt'\n\nresp = SESSION.get(CLIMATE_URL, timeout=15)\nresp.raise_for_status()\nprint(f\"Met Office climate series: HTTP {resp.status_code}, {len(resp.text)} characters\")\n\nlines = resp.text.strip().split('\\n')\nprint(\"Header (first 5 lines):\")\nfor line in lines[:5]:\n    print(\" \", line)\n\n# The data rows start once a line begins with a 4-digit year\ndata_lines = [ln for ln in lines if re.match(r'^\\s*\\d{4}\\s', ln)]\nprint(f\"\\nData rows detected: {len(data_lines)} (years {data_lines[0].split()[0]}-{data_lines[-1].split()[0]})\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:13:41.143868Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:13:41.144287Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:13:41.265841Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:13:41.144256Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:13:41.26454Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Met Office climate series: HTTP 200, 18852 characters\nHeader (first 5 lines):\n  Areal values from HadUK-Grid 1km gridded climate data from land surface network\n  Source: Met Office National Climate Information Centre\n  Monthly, seasonal and annual mean air temperature for England SE and Central S\n  Areal series, starting in 1884\n  Last updated 01-Sep-2026 11:57\n\nData rows detected: 143 (years 1884-2026)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":4},{"id":"b7852f0d","cell_type":"code","source":"cols = ['year', 'jan', 'feb', 'mar', 'apr', 'may', 'jun', 'jul', 'aug', 'sep', 'oct', 'nov', 'dec',\n        'win', 'spr', 'sum', 'aut', 'ann']\n\n# This file is fixed-width, not whitespace-delimited: a naive line.split() silently misaligns\n# any row with trailing missing months (2026, still in progress, is exactly such a row), because\n# it has no way to tell \"value absent\" apart from \"next value starts here\". We derive the true\n# column boundaries from the header line itself and parse with a fixed-width reader instead.\nheader_idx = next(i for i, ln in enumerate(lines) if ln.strip().startswith('year'))\nheader_line = lines[header_idx]\n# Field boundaries sit at the END of each header label, not the start: these are right-aligned\n# numeric columns, so a wide value like \"20.7\" needs room to extend left of where its label began.\nends = [m.end() for m in re.finditer(r'\\S+', header_line)]\ncolspecs = [(0, ends[0])] + list(zip(ends[:-1], ends[1:]))\n\nclimate = pd.read_fwf(StringIO(resp.text), colspecs=colspecs, names=cols,\n                       skiprows=header_idx + 1, na_values=['---'])\nclimate['year'] = climate['year'].astype(int)\nclimate = climate.set_index('year')\n\nprint(f\"Parsed climate series: {climate.index.min()}-{climate.index.max()}, {len(climate)} years\")\nprint(f\"Missing annual means: {climate['ann'].isna().sum()} year(s) \"\n      f\"({climate[climate['ann'].isna()].index.tolist()})\")\nclimate.tail(8)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:13:41.485766Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:13:41.486173Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:13:41.52642Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:13:41.486141Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:13:41.525163Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Parsed climate series: 1884-2026, 143 years\nMissing annual m