ize:17px;\">&#128270;</span> A fixed-width file is not a whitespace-delimited file</div>\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.75;\">\nThe temptation with a file that looks like simple space-separated columns is to just call\nline.split() and zip the results against the column names. That works right up until a row is\nmissing values in the middle rather than at the end, which is exactly what happens to whichever year\nis still in progress: 2026 arrives with January through August filled in, September through December\ngenuinely blank, and the three completed seasonal columns (winter, spring, summer) present after that\ngap. A naive split() has no concept of a gap; it just takes the eleven numbers it can find and assigns\nthem to the first eleven column slots, quietly relabelling the winter, spring and summer values as\nSeptember, October and November. The fix is to parse the file for what it actually is, fixed-width\ntext, using the header line's own column boundaries rather than counting tokens, which places every\nvalue in its correct column whether or not the row has gaps.</div>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"7fa086a7","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"sec4\"></a>\n<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#450a0a 0%,#7f1d1d 60%,#b91c1c 100%);padding:26px 32px;border-radius:14px;margin:34px 0 20px 0;box-shadow:0 6px 24px rgba(69,10,10,0.25);\">\n<div style=\"color:rgba(255,255,255,0.55);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:4px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:8px;\">Section 4</div>\n<h2 style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:24px;margin:0;font-weight:700;\">Exploratory Data Analysis: The UK Cross-Section</h2>\n<p style=\"color:#f0c9c9;margin:6px 0 0 0;font-size:13.5px;\">Twelve views of what seventy-six cities looked like at the same moment</p>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"f7185c25","cell_type":"markdown","source":"\nBefore any spatial statistic is computed formally, it is worth simply looking at what the live