ata","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 950x600 with 1 Axes>","image/png":""},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":20},{"id":"254becf7","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#f5f5f4,#e7e5e4);border:1px solid #78716c44;border-left:5px solid #78716c;border-radius:0 12px 12px 0;padding:18px 24px;margin:14px 0;\">\n<div style=\"font-weight:700;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;font-size:14px;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;\"><span style=\"font-size:17px;\">&#128270;</span> Reading Plot 12</div>\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.75;\">\nDay length is not weather in the usual sense, it follows from the date and the earth's axial tilt with\nno atmospheric randomness at all, which makes it a useful sanity check rather than a finding: the slope\nhere should come out positive and essentially deterministic if the sunrise and sunset fields pulled from\nthe API are being read correctly, since any point in the UK further north than another sees a longer day\nfor as long as the notebook is run after the spring equinox and before the autumn one, purely as a\nmatter of geometry.</div>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"2d05f60b","cell_type":"code","source":"fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 8))\nnation_counts = weather['Nation'].value_counts()\nwedges, texts, autotexts = ax.pie(\n    nation_counts.values, labels=nation_counts.index, colors=[NATION_COLORS[n] for n in nation_counts.index],\n    autopct=lambda p: f'{p:.0f}%\\n({p*sum(nation_counts.values)/100:.0f})', startangle=90,\n    wedgeprops={'edgecolor': 'white', 'linewidth': 2}, textprops={'color': RUST, 'fontsize': 10})\nfor at in autotexts:\n    at.set_color('white'); at.set_fontweight('bold')\nax.set_title(f'Plot 12b. Sample composition by nation (n={len(weather)})', fontsize=13, color=RUST, fontweight='bold')\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:18:23.543014Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:18:23.543387Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:18:23.799938Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:18:23.54336Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:18:23.79902Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 800x800 with 1 Axes>","image/png":""},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":21},{"id":"dbc55695","cell_type":"code","source":"fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(11, 7.5))\nsizes = 15 + (weather['wind_speed_ms'] / weather['wind_speed_ms'].max()) * 400\nsc = ax.scatter(weather['temp_C'], weather['humidity_pct'], s=sizes, c=weather['pressure_hpa'],\n                 cmap='Reds', alpha=0.7, edgecolor=RUST, linewidth=0.6)\nfor _, row in weather.nlargest(5, 'wind_speed_ms').iterrows():\n    ax.annotate(row['City'], (row['temp_C'], row['humidity_pct']), fontsize=8, color=RUST,\n                xytext=(5, 5), textcoords='offset points', fontweight='bold')\ncbar = plt.colorbar(sc, ax=ax); cbar.set_label('Pressure (hPa)', color=RUST)\nhandles = [plt.scatter([], [], s=15 + f*400, color=GREY_MID, edgecolor=RUST, alpha=0.7, label=f'{f*weather[\"wind_speed_ms\"].max():.0f} m/s')\n           for f in [0.1, 0.4, 0.8]]\nax.legend(handles=handles, title='Wind speed', frameon=False, fontsize=8.5, loc='upper right')\nax.set_title('Plot 12c. Temperature, humidity, pressure and wind speed together',\n              fontsize=13, color=RUST, fontweight='bold')\nax.set_xlabel('Temperature (\\u00b0C)'); ax.set_ylabel('Humidity (%)')\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:18:23.978362Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:18:23.978772Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:18:24.556005Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:18:23.978743Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:18:24.55458Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1100x750 with 2 Axes>","image/png":""},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":22},{"id":"0ce664e2","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#f5f5f4,#e7e5e4);border:1px solid #78716c44;border-left:5px solid #78716c;border-radius:0 12px 12px 0;padding:18px 24px;margin:14px 0;\">\n<div style=\"font-weight:700;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;font-size:14px;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;\"><span style=\"font-size:17px;\">&#128270;</span> Reading Plots 12b and 12c</div>\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.75;\">\nThe pie chart is a composition check as much as a finding, England's share should sit close to its\nshare of real UK cities on the government's list (roughly seventy percent), and a badly skewed slice\nhere would be the first sign of a geocoding problem upstream in Section 3.1 rather than anything about\nthe weather itself. The bubble chart earns its place by carrying four variables at once without a fifth\npanel: the labelled cities are the five windiest in the sample, and if they cluster toward the\nlower-pressure end of the colour scale as a group, that is the low-pressure-drives-wind relationship\nPlot 4's correlation heatmap already reported, now visible directly on specific, named cities rather\nthan as an abstract correlation coefficient.</div>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"545a6304","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"sec5\"></a>\n<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#450a0a 0%,#7f1d1d 60%,#b91c1c 100%);padding:26px 32px;border-radius:14px;margin:34px 0 20px 0;box-shadow:0 6px 24px rgba(69,10,10,0.25);\">\n<div style=\"color:rgba(255,255,255,0.55);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:4px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:8px;\">Section 5</div>\n<h2 style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:24px;margin:0;font-weight:700;\">Spatial Statistical Modeling</h2>\n<p style=\"color:#f0c9c9;margin:6px 0 0 0;font-size:13.5px;\">Does geography actually structure the current UK weather cross-section, and if so, how</p>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"d71d53c3","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#dc2626 0%,#7f1d1d 100%);border-radius:8px;padding:11px 20px;margin:24px 0 14px 0;\">\n<span style=\"color:#fecaca;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;\">5.1</span>\n<span style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">Moran's I: Is There Spatial Autocorrelation at All</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"ded0d488","cell_type":"markdown","source":"\nBefore fitting anything, Section 2.1's question needs an answer: does the temperature at one city carry\ninformation about temperature at nearby cities, over and above the country-wide average? We use a\nk-nearest-neighbour spatial weights matrix, each city's five nearest neighbours by great-circle distance,\nrather than a fixed-radius neighbourhood, since city density varies a great deal between, for instance,\nthe Home Counties and the Scottish Highlands.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"8653b6f9","cell_type":"code","source":"from libpysal.weights import KNN\nfrom esda.moran import Moran\n\ncoords = weather[['lon', 'lat']].values\nw = KNN.from_array(coords, k=5)\nw.transform = 'r'\n\nmoran_temp = Moran(weather['temp_C'].values, w, permutations=999)\nprint(f\"Moran's I (temperature) = {moran_temp.I:.4f}  (expectation under randomness: {moran_temp.EI:.4f})\")\nprint(f\"Permutation-based p-value: {moran_temp.p_sim:.4f}  (z = {moran_temp.z_sim:.3f})\")\n\nmoran_table = pd.DataFrame({\n    'Variable': ['temp_C', 'humidity_pct', 'pressure_hpa', 'wind_speed_ms'],\n    \"Moran's I\": [Moran(weather[c].values, w, permutations=999).I for c in\n                  ['temp_C', 'humidity_pct', 'pressure_hpa', 'wind_speed_ms']],\n    'p-value (permutation)': [Moran(weather[c].values, w, permutations=999).p_sim for c in\n                               ['temp_C', 'humidity_pct', 'pressure_hpa', 'wind_speed_ms']],\n})\nmoran_table","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:18:28.335033Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:18:28.335425Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:18:32.450232Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:18:28.335397Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:18:32.448839Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Moran's I (temperature) = 0.8604  (expectation under randomness: -0.0133)\nPermutation-based p-value: 0.0010  (z = 13.361)\n","output_type":"stream"},{"name":"stderr","text":"/opt/conda/lib/python3.10/site-packages/libpysal/weights/weights.py:224: UserWarning: The weights matrix is not fully connected: \n There are 2 disconnected components.\n  warnings.warn(message)\n","output_type":"stream"},{"execution_count":23,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"        Variable  Moran's I  p-value (permutation)\n0         temp_C   0.860391                  0.001\n1   humidity_pct   0.334773                  0.001\n2   pressure_hpa   0.895460                  0.001\n3  wind_speed_ms   0.186069                  0.003","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>Variable</th>\n      <th>Moran's I</th>\n      <th>p-value (permutation)</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>temp_C</td>\n      <td>0.860391</td>\n      <td>0.001</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>humidity_pct</td>\n      <td>0.334773</td>\n      <td>0.001</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>pressure_hpa</td>\n      <td>0.895460</td>\n      <td>0.001</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>3</th>\n      <td>wind_speed_ms</td>\n      <td>0.186069</td>\n      <td>0.003</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":23},{"id":"91d2ec7e","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fef2f2;border-radius:14px;padding:22px 28px;border:1.5px solid #fca5a5;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.8;color:#7f1d1d;margin:14px 0;\">\n<h4 style=\"color:#b91c1c;margin:0 0 10px;font-size:1.0rem;\">A warning worth reading, not silencing</h4>\n\nThis cell prints a UserWarning: <em>\"The weights matrix is not fully connected: there are 2 disconnected\ncomponents.\"</em> That is libpysal noticing something geographically true rather than a bug to suppress.\nA k-nearest-neighbour graph built from five neighbours each connects Northern Ireland's cities to their\nfive closest neighbours, which are each other, and Great Britain's cities to theirs, but nothing forces\nan edge to cross the Irish Sea, since every Northern Irish city's five nearest cities are all still in\nNorthern Ireland. The result is two separate connected pieces rather than one continuous graph. Moran's\nI is still computable and still valid on a disconnected weights matrix, it just means the test's null\nhypothesis of spatial randomness is being evaluated over two islands worth of adjacency rather than one\nfully joined surface, worth knowing before reading too much into any single cross-Irish-Sea comparison\nspecifically.\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"b0e23b7c","cell_type":"code","source":"from esda.moran import Moran_Local\n\nlisa = Moran_Local(weather['temp_C'].values, w, permutations=999)\nweather['lisa_quadrant'] = lisa.q\nweather['lisa_significant'] = lisa.p_sim < 0.05\n\nquad_labels = {1: 'High-High (warm cluster)', 2: 'Low-High (cold outlier)',\n               3: 'Low-Low (cold cluster)', 4: 'High-Low (warm outlier)'}\nquad_colors = {1: RUST, 2: GREY_MID, 3: GREY_DARK, 4: BURNT}\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(9, 11))\nuk_outline.plot(ax=ax, color='#f0efee', edgecolor='white', linewidth=0.8)\nfor q, label in quad_labels.items():\n    sub = weather[(weather['lisa_quadrant'] == q) & weather['lisa_significant']]\n    ax.scatter(sub['lon'], sub['lat'], color=quad_colors[q], s=100, edgecolor='white',\n               linewidth=0.8, label=f'{label} (n={len(sub)})', zorder=3)\ninsig = weather[~weather['lisa_significant']]\nax.scatter(insig['lon'], insig['lat'], color='#d6d3d1', s=50, edgecolor='white', label='not significant', zorder=2)\nax.set_xlim(-8.5, 2); ax.set_ylim(49.8, 60.9); ax.set_axis_off()\nax.set_title(\"Plot 13. Local indicators of spatial association (LISA), temperature\", fontsize=13,\n             color=RUST, fontweight='bold')\nax.legend(frameon=False, fontsize=8, loc='upper left')\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:18:36.093612Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:18:36.094033Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:18:43.756474Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:18:36.093993Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:18:43.755099Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 900x1100 with 1 Axes>","image/png":""},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":24},{"id":"df329545","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fef2f2;border-radius:12px;padding:20px 26px;border-left:5px solid #b91c1c;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin:14px 0;\">\n<h4 style=\"color:#7f1d1d;margin:0 0 10px;\">Results: strong, genuine spatial autocorrelation</h4>\n\nRead the printed statistic together with its permutation p-value rather than trusting the interpretation\nbelow to match today's exact decimal: this is live weather, so the precise value of Moran's I moves from\nrun to run as the underlying snapshot changes. What has stayed consistent across repeated runs is the\npattern, not the digits. Temperature comes back with I comfortably above 0.7, against an expectation of\nessentially zero under spatial randomness, with a permutation p-value at the smallest value the 999\npermutations used here can even report (0.001) and a z-score in the double digits, about as unambiguous\na rejection of \"temperature is scattered randomly across the country\" as a real dataset is likely to\nproduce. Pressure has come back even more strongly autocorrelated than temperature on every run so far,\nwhich makes physical sense, barometric pressure varies on the scale of whole weather systems hundreds of\nkilometres across, so neighbouring cities are almost always inside the same system. Humidity sits\nconsistently in between, and wind speed is the one variable whose significance is worth watching rather\nthan assuming: it is the most locally noisy of the four, shaped by small-scale terrain and obstruction as\nmuch as by the broader weather pattern, and its p-value has landed on both sides of the conventional 0.05\nline across different runs, a real reminder that \"significant\" is a property of a specific sample, not a\npermanent fact about a variable. The LISA map is the part of this result least sensitive to any single\nrun: it shows the autocorrelation is not a diffuse statistical artefact but resolves into specific,\ngeographically coherent hot and cold clusters, which is the stronger and more convincing version of H1\nthan the global statistic alone.\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"5379645e","cell_type":"markdown","source":"\nSection 2.1 raised the natural follow-up question directly: does the choice of k = 5 actually matter?\nA sensitivity check answers it by refitting the weights matrix across a range of k, tracking both the\nresulting Moran's I and, following the connectivity idea from Section 2.1, exactly how many separate\npieces the graph breaks into at each value.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"4d82a57c","cell_type":"code","source":"import warnings\n\nk_values = [3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 15]\nsensitivity_rows = []\nfor k in k_values:\n    with warnings.catch_warnings(record=True) as caught:\n        warnings.simplefilter('always')\n        w_k = KNN.from_array(coords, k=k)\n        w_k.transform = 'r'\n        n_components = getattr(w_k, 'n_components', np.nan)\n    mi_k = Moran(weather['temp_C'].values, w_k, permutations=999)\n    sensitivity_rows.append({'k': k, \"Moran's I\": mi_k.I, 'p-value': mi_k.p_sim,\n                              'Connected components': n_components})\n\nsensitivity_table = pd.DataFrame(sensitivity_rows)\nmin_connected_k = sensitivity_table.loc[sensitivity_table['Connected components'] == 1, 'k'].min()\nprint(f\"Smallest k giving a single connected component: {min_connected_k}\")\nsensitivity_table","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:18:47.941949Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:18:47.942392Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:18:48.141398Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:18:47.942361Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:18:48.13975Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Smallest k giving a single connected component: 8\n","output_type":"stream"},{"execution_count":25,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"    k  Moran's I  p-value  Connected components\n0   3   0.864235    0.001                     2\n1   4   0.849735    0.001                     2\n2   5   0.860391    0.001                     2\n3   6   0.865006    0.001                     2\n4   8   0.859332    0.001                     1\n5  10   0.790777    0.001                     1\n6  12   0.756084    0.001                     1\n7  15   0.688367    0.001                     1","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>k</th>\n      <th>Moran's I</th>\n      <th>p-value</th>\n      <th>Connected components</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>3</td>\n      <td>0.864235</td>\n      <td>0.001</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>4</td>\n      <td>0.849735</td>\n      <td>0.001</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>0.860391</td>\n      <td>0.001</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>3</th>\n      <td>6</td>\n      <td>0.865006</td>\n      <td>0.001</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>4</th>\n      <td>8</td>\n      <td>0.859332</td>\n      <td>0.001</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>5</th>\n      <td>10</td>\n      <td>0.790777</td>\n      <td>0.001</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>6</th>\n      <td>12</td>\n      <td>0.756084</td>\n      <td>0.001</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>7</th>\n      <td>15</td>\n      <td>0.688367</td>\n      <td>0.001</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":25},{"id":"dd9d64d7","cell_type":"code","source":"fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(14, 5))\naxes[0].plot(sensitivity_table['k'], sensitivity_table[\"Moran's I\"], color=RUST, marker='o', markersize=7, linewidth=2)\naxes[0].axvline(min_connected_k, color=GREY_DARK, linestyle='--', linewidth=1.5,\n                label=f'graph fully connected from k={min_connected_k}')\naxes[0].axvline(5, color=BURNT, linestyle=':', linewidth=1.5, label='k=5 (used above)')\naxes[0].set_title(\"Plot 13b. Moran's I across candidate k\", fontsize=12.5, color=RUST, fontweight='bold')\naxes[0].set_xlabel('k (nearest neighbours)'); axes[0].set_ylabel(\"Moran's I\")\naxes[0].legend(frameon=False, fontsize=8.5)\n\naxes[1].bar(sensitivity_table['k'].astype(str), sensitivity_table['Connected components'],\n            color=GREY_MID, edgecolor=RUST)\naxes[1].set_title('Plot 13c. Connected components by k', fontsize=12.5, color=RUST, fontweight='bold')\naxes[1].set_xlabel('k'); axes[1].set_ylabel('Number of connected components')\naxes[1].set_yticks(range(0, int(sensitivity_table['Connected components'].max()) + 2))\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:18:49.462243Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:18:49.462668Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:18:49.957761Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:18:49.462627Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:18:49.956445Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1400x500 with 2 Axes>","image/png":""},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":26},{"id":"63532e80","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fef2f2;border-radius:12px;padding:20px 26px;border-left:5px solid #b91c1c;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin:14px 0;\">\n<h4 style=\"color:#7f1d1d;margin:0 0 10px;\">Results: k matters for connectivity, barely matters for the finding itself</h4>\n\nThe graph stays split into two pieces, Great Britain and Northern Ireland, for every k below the value\nprinted above, and becomes a single connected surface only once k is large enough that at least one\nNorthern Irish city's neighbour list reaches across the water to a Great British one. Moran's I itself\nmoves far less across this whole range than it would need to for the substantive conclusion to change:\nevery value from k = 3 to k = 15 has stayed comfortably positive and clearly significant on every run so\nfar, which is the honest, and reassuring, answer to whether k = 5 was doing unacknowledged work in\nSection 5.1's headline result: it was not. This is the closest analogue available to a silhouette-style\nselection criterion for a spatial weights matrix rather than a clustering: silhouette scores measure how\nwell a partition separates groups that do not have to be geographically contiguous, while a\nk-nearest-neighbour graph's own connectivity measures something more basic and more directly checkable,\nwhether the neighbourhood definition itself is geographically sound before any statistic is computed on\ntop of it. Both are, in the end, the same kind of question asked of different structures: does this\nchoice of hyperparameter produce something coherent, or something that only looks coherent by accident.\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"5a2c2766","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#dc2626 0%,#7f1d1d 100%);border-radius:8px;padding:11px 20px;margin:24px 0 14px 0;\">\n<span style=\"color:#fecaca;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;\">5.2</span>\n<span style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">What Predicts Current Temperature: Latitude and the Coast</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"0bd56922","cell_type":"markdown","source":"\nH2 makes two separate claims: latitude should predict temperature negatively, and coastal cities should\nbehave differently from inland ones. We build a simple proxy for coastal exposure, distance from each\ncity to the nearest point on the UK coastline, and test both in a single regression.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"e5802053","cell_type":"code","source":"from shapely.ops import unary_union\nfrom shapely.geometry import Point\n\nuk_boundary = unary_union(uk_outline.geometry)\ncoastline = uk_boundary.boundary\n\ndef km_to_coast(lon, lat):\n    # rough planar approximation: fine at UK latitudes for a descriptive distance measure\n    nearest = coastline.interpolate(coastline.project(Point(lon, lat)))\n    dx = (lon - nearest.x) * 111.32 * np.cos(np.radians(lat))\n    dy = (lat - nearest.y) * 110.57\n    return float(np.hypot(dx, dy))\n\nweather['dist_coast_km'] = [km_to_coast(lo, la) for lo, la in zip(weather['lon'], weather['lat'])]\n\nimport statsmodels.api as sm\nX = sm.add_constant(weather[['lat', 'dist_coast_km']])\nspatial_model = sm.OLS(weather['temp_C'], X).fit(cov_type='HC1')\nprint(spatial_model.summary())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:18:54.650946Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:18:54.651328Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:18:56.594543Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:18:54.6513Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:18:56.593064Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"                            OLS Regression Results                            \n==============================================================================\nDep. Variable:                 temp_C   R-squared:                       0.641\nModel:                            OLS   Adj. R-squared:                  0.631\nMethod:                 Least Squares   F-statistic:                     80.54\nDate:                Sat, 12 Sep 2026   Prob (F-statistic):           3.38e-19\nTime:                        19:18:56   Log-Likelihood:                -107.78\nNo. Observations:                  76   AIC:                             221.6\nDf Residuals:                      73   BIC:                             228.5\nDf Model:                           2                                         \nCovariance Type:                  HC1                                         \n=================================================================================\n                    coef    std err          z      P>|z|      [0.025      0.975]\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nconst            56.2862      3.387     16.619      0.000      49.648      62.924\nlat              -0.7315      0.063    -11.586      0.000      -0.855      -0.608\ndist_coast_km     0.0072      0.003      2.819      0.005       0.002       0.012\n==============================================================================\nOmnibus:                        4.299   Durbin-Watson:                   1.756\nProb(Omnibus):                  0.117   Jarque-Bera (JB):                4.212\nSkew:                           0.569   Prob(JB):                        0.122\nKurtosis:                       2.811   Cond. No.                     2.63e+03\n==============================================================================\n\nNotes:\n[1] Standard Errors are heteroscedasticity robust (HC1)\n[2] The condition number is large, 2.63e+03. This might indicate that there are\nstrong multicollinearity or other numerical problems.\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":27},{"id":"91738e3c","cell_type":"code","source":"fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(14, 5.5))\naxes[0].scatter(weather['dist_coast_km'], weather['temp_C'], color=RUST, s=60, alpha=0.8, edgecolor='white')\nz = np.polyfit(weather['dist_coast_km'], weather['temp_C'], 1)\nxs = np.linspace(weather['dist_coast_km'].min(), weather['dist_coast_km'].max(), 20)\naxes[0].plot(xs, np.polyval(z, xs), color=GREY_DARK, linestyle='--', linewidth=2.2)\naxes[0].set_title('Plot 14a. Temperature vs distance to coast', fontsize=12, color=RUST, fontweight='bold')\naxes[0].set_xlabel('Distance to coast (km)'); axes[0].set_ylabel('Temperature (\\u00b0C)')\n\nresid = spatial_model.resid\naxes[1].scatter(spatial_model.fittedvalues, resid, color=BURNT, alpha=0.7, s=50, edgecolor='white')\naxes[1].axhline(0, color=RUST, linewidth=1)\naxes[1].set_title('Plot 14b. Residuals vs fitted, spatial regression', fontsize=12, color=RUST, fontweight='bold')\naxes[1].set_xlabel('Fitted temperature'); axes[1].set_ylabel('Residual')\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:19:02.93355Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:19:02.934205Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:19:03.466406Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:19:02.934168Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:19:03.465041Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1400x550 with 2 Axes>","image/png":""},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":28},{"id":"afb212ec","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fef2f2;border-radius:12px;padding:20px 26px;border-left:5px solid #b91c1c;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin:14px 0;\">\n<h4 style=\"color:#7f1d1d;margin:0 0 10px;\">Results: latitude confirmed, coastal distance the wrong sign to tell the simple story</h4>\n\nAs with Section 5.1, treat the specific coefficients printed above as this run's numbers rather than\nfixed constants, this is a live snapshot and a different hour or day will move them, though not the\npattern reported here across repeated runs. Latitude comes back negative and significant at p &lt;\n0.001 on every run so far: moving one degree further north has cost somewhere between half a degree and three quarters of a\ndegree Celsius across runs so far, a clean confirmation of H2's first half, over a sample spanning roughly Truro to\nInverness. The two predictors together have consistently explained a substantial share of the variance in\ntemperature, typically somewhere around half and occasionally more, a respectable amount for a\nsingle-snapshot cross-section built from only two variables.\n<br><br>\nThe coastal-distance coefficient is where the finding gets more interesting than the hypothesis\nanticipated, and this part of the pattern has also held up across runs: it comes back positive and\nsignificant, meaning cities further from the coast were, on average, slightly <em>warmer</em>, not\ncooler. This is the opposite sign from the simple \"the sea moderates temperature, so coastal cities are\ncooler\" story, and it is worth taking seriously rather than explaining away, because it points to a\nreal, well-documented mechanism this single-snapshot design cannot fully separate from the alternative:\nland loses heat faster than the sea after sunset. Every run so far has been fetched in the early evening,\npast peak daytime heating, when coastal areas, buffered by the sea's slower cooling, would be expected to\nhold their daytime warmth a little longer than inland areas that are already dropping temperature into\nthe evening, exactly the mechanism that gives coastal regions a <em>smaller</em> diurnal range rather\nthan uniformly cooler temperatures at every hour. A morning snapshot could plausibly show the\ncoefficient's sign flip entirely, and if this notebook is ever re-run at a very different time of day,\nthat specific prediction is directly checkable against whatever the new coefficient turns out to be.\nThis is precisely why H2's second half was phrased around diurnal range rather than a constant sign, and\nprecisely what Section 9.2 means when it says a single snapshot cannot separate a moderating effect from\nwhatever one particular day's timing happened to be doing.\n<br><br>\nTwo secondary diagnostics are worth a mention rather than a worry: statsmodels tends to flag a large\ncondition number here, a joint symptom of latitude and distance-to-coast varying on very different\nnumeric scales rather than genuine collinearity between them, since the two are conceptually distinct\nmeasures of geography rather than restatements of each other, and the Jarque-Bera test's exact p-value\nhas moved between comfortably insignificant and borderline across runs without ever suggesting a skew\nsevere enough to overturn either coefficient's sign or significance.\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"5e600959","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#dc2626 0%,#7f1d1d 100%);border-radius:8px;padding:11px 20px;margin:24px 0 14px 0;\">\n<span style=\"color:#fecaca;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;\">5.3</span>\n<span style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">Correcting for the Spatial Autocorrelation This Regression Ignores</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"833569b6","cell_type":"markdown","source":"\nSection 5.1 already found strong spatial autocorrelation in raw temperature. An ordinary least squares\nregression assumes its errors are independent across observations, an assumption Section 5.1 gives\ndirect reason to doubt for this specific outcome variable. The honest next step is not to stop at the\nOLS table above, but to check whether its own residuals still carry spatial structure, and if they do,\nto refit with a model built for exactly that.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"5b90561a","cell_type":"code","source":"from spreg import OLS as SpregOLS\n\ny = weather[['temp_C']].values\nX_spreg = weather[['lat', 'dist_coast_km']].values\n\nols_spreg = SpregOLS(y, X_spreg, w=w, spat_diag=True, moran=True,\n                      name_y='temp_C', name_x=['lat', 'dist_coast_km'])\nmoran_stat, moran_z, moran_p = ols_spreg.moran_res\nprint(f\"Moran's I on the OLS residuals: {moran_stat:.4f}  (z = {moran_z:.3f}, p = {moran_p:.4f})\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:19:07.902901Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:19:07.903329Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:19:08.010369Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:19:07.903299Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:19:08.009192Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Moran's I on the OLS residuals: 0.6172  (z = 10.525, p = 0.0000)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":29},{"id":"fd572885","cell_type":"code","source":"from spreg import GM_Lag, GM_Error_Het\n\nlag_model = GM_Lag(y, X_spreg, w=w, name_y='temp_C', name_x=['lat', 'dist_coast_km'])\nerror_model = GM_Error_Het(y, X_spreg, w=w, name_y='temp_C', name_x=['lat', 'dist_coast_km'])\n\nspatial_comparison = pd.DataFrame({\n    'Model': ['OLS (Section 5.2)', 'Spatial Lag (GM)', 'Spatial Error (GM, heteroskedastic)'],\n    'Coef: latitude': [spatial_model.params['lat'], lag_model.betas[1][0], error_model.betas[1][0]],\n    'Coef: dist_coast_km': [spatial_model.params['dist_coast_km'], lag_model.betas[2][0], error_model.betas[2][0]],\n    'Spatial parameter': [np.nan, lag_model.rho[0], error_model.betas[-1][0]],\n    'Pseudo-R\\u00b2': [spatial_model.rsquared, lag_model.pr2, np.nan],\n})\nspatial_comparison","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:19:08.557448Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:19:08.558138Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:19:08.659231Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:19:08.558095Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:19:08.658065Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":30,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"                                 Model  Coef: latitude  Coef: dist_coast_km  \\\n0                    OLS (Section 5.2)       -0.731479             0.007241   \n1                     Spatial Lag (GM)       -0.216827             0.003773   \n2  Spatial Error (GM, heteroskedastic)       -0.489277             0.004014   \n\n   Spatial parameter  Pseudo-R²  \n0                NaN   0.640855  \n1           0.726519   0.870083  \n2           0.814304        NaN  ","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>Model</th>\n      <th>Coef: latitude</th>\n      <th>Coef: dist_coast_km</th>\n      <th>Spatial parameter</th>\n      <th>Pseudo-R²</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>OLS (Section 5.2)</td>\n      <td>-0.731479</td>\n      <td>0.007241</td>\n      <td>NaN</td>\n      <td>0.640855</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>Spatial Lag (GM)</td>\n      <td>-0.216827</td>\n      <td>0.003773</td>\n      <td>0.726519</td>\n      <td>0.870083</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>Spatial Error (GM, heteroskedastic)</td>\n      <td>-0.489277</td>\n      <td>0.004014</td>\n      <td>0.814304</td>\n      <td>NaN</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":30},{"id":"b6d67575","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fef2f2;border-radius:12px;padding:20px 26px;border-left:5px solid #b91c1c;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin:14px 0;\">\n<h4 style=\"color:#7f1d1d;margin:0 0 10px;\">Results: what changes once space is modelled directly</h4>\n\nIf the OLS residual Moran's I above comes out small and non-significant, that is itself informative:\nit means latitude and coastal distance, simple as they are, already absorb most of the spatial structure\nin temperature, and the un-corrected OLS coefficients in Section 5.2 were not meaningfully biased by\nignoring space. If instead the residual Moran's I is still significant, the spatial lag model's\n&rho; coefficient quantifies exactly how much of that leftover structure comes from neighbouring cities'\ntemperatures directly influencing each other's fitted value (a spillover story, plausible for weather\nsystems that span multiple cities at once), while the spatial error model's coefficient attributes it\ninstead to spatially correlated omitted factors the regression has not measured. The comparison table's\nreal value is not picking a winner between three rows of similar-looking coefficients, it is checking\nthat the latitude and coastal-distance findings from Section 5.2 survive being modelled properly, which\nis a substantially higher bar than an OLS table that never checked its own residuals for the exact\nproblem Section 5.1 had already flagged.\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"73a86a86","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"sec6\"></a>\n<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#450a0a 0%,#7f1d1d 60%,#b91c1c 100%);padding:26px 32px;border-radius:14px;margin:34px 0 20px 0;box-shadow:0 6px 24px rgba(69,10,10,0.25);\">\n<div style=\"color:rgba(255,255,255,0.55);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:4px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:8px;\">Section 6</div>\n<h2 style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:24px;margin:0;font-weight:700;\">Exploratory Analysis: A Century of England's Climate</h2>\n<p style=\"color:#f0c9c9;margin:6px 0 0 0;font-size:13.5px;\">First look at 142 years of annual and monthly mean temperature</p>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"19104590","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#dc2626 0%,#7f1d1d 100%);border-radius:8px;padding:11px 20px;margin:24px 0 14px 0;\">\n<span style=\"color:#fecaca;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;\">6.1</span>\n<span style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">The Series Itself</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"27ed3d4f","cell_type":"code","source":"annual = climate['ann'].dropna()\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(13, 6.5))\nax.plot(annual.index, annual.values, color=GREY_MID, linewidth=1.1, alpha=0.7, label='Annual mean')\nax.plot(annual.index, annual.rolling(10, center=True).mean(), color=RUST, linewidth=2.6, label='10-year rolling mean')\nax.axhline(annual.loc[1961:1990].mean(), color=GREY_DARK, linestyle='--', linewidth=1.5, label='1961-1990 baseline')\nax.set_title('Plot 15. Annual mean temperature, South East and Central South England, 1884-2025',\n             fontsize=13, color=RUST, fontweight='bold')\nax.set_ylabel('Temperature (\\u00b0C)')\nax.legend(frameon=False, fontsize=9.5)\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:19:12.450913Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:19:12.451392Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:19:12.936756Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:19:12.451361Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:19:12.935235Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1300x650 with 1 Axes>","image/png":""},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":31},{"id":"f2ea11b6","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#f5f5f4,#e7e5e4);border:1px solid #78716c44;border-left:5px solid #78716c;border-radius:0 12px 12px 0;padding:18px 24px;margin:14px 0;\">\n<div style=\"font-weight:700;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;font-size:14px;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;\"><span style=\"font-size:17px;\">&#128270;</span> Reading Plot 15</div>\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.75;\">\nThe raw annual series is noisy enough that no trend would be obvious from it alone, individual years\nswing by more than a degree either side of their neighbours, which is exactly why Section 7.3 reaches\nfor a formal test rather than an eyeballed slope. The ten-year rolling mean strips most of that noise\nout, and what is left climbs visibly and fairly steadily above the 1961-1990 baseline from around the\n1990s onward, without ever returning to it. Whether that climb is a statistically real trend rather than\na long, slow swing is precisely the question Section 7 answers directly.</div>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"c2e0d3dc","cell_type":"code","source":"months = ['jan','feb','mar','apr','may','jun','jul','aug','sep','oct','nov','dec']\nmonth_names = ['Jan','Feb','Mar','Apr','May','Jun','Jul','Aug','Sep','Oct','Nov','Dec']\nmonthly_stats = climate[months].agg(['mean', 'std', 'min', 'max']).T\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(11, 6))\nax.plot(month_names, monthly_stats['mean'], color=RUST, linewidth=2.4, marker='o', markersize=6)\nax.fill_between(range(12), monthly_stats['mean'] - monthly_stats['std'], monthly_stats['mean'] + monthly_stats['std'],\n                color=GREY_MID, alpha=0.3, label='\\u00b1 1 standard deviation')\nax.set_title('Plot 16. Average seasonal cycle, 1884-2025', fontsize=13, color=RUST, fontweight='bold')\nax.set_ylabel('Temperature (\\u00b0C)')\nax.legend(frameon=False, fontsize=9.5)\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:19:15.30317Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:19:15.303535Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:19:15.851719Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:19:15.303508Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:19:15.850099Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1100x600 with 1 Axes>","image/png":""},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":32},{"id":"f77d03bd","cell_type":"code","source":"fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(11, 6))\nmonthly_long = climate[months].reset_index().melt(id_vars='year', var_name='month', value_name='temp')\nmonthly_long['month'] = pd.Categorical(monthly_long['month'], categories=months, ordered=True)\nbp_data = [monthly_long.loc[monthly_long['month'] == m, 'temp'].dropna() for m in months]\nbp = ax.boxplot(bp_data, patch_artist=True, widths=0.6)\nax.set_xticklabels(month_names)\nfor patch in bp['boxes']:\n    patch.set_facecolor(GREY_MID); patch.set_alpha(0.7)\nax.set_title('Plot 17. Monthly temperature distributions, all 142 years', fontsize=13, color=RUST, fontweight='bold')\nax.set_ylabel('Temperature (\\u00b0C)')\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:20:02.92811Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:20:02.928749Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:20:03.443318Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:20:02.928697Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:20:03.4416Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1100x600 with 1 Axes>","image/png":""},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":34},{"id":"d78e726f","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#f5f5f4,#e7e5e4);border:1px solid #78716c44;border-left:5px solid #78716c;border-radius:0 12px 12px 0;padding:18px 24px;margin:14px 0;\">\n<div style=\"font-weight:700;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;font-size:14px;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;\"><span style=\"font-size:17px;\">&#128270;</span> Reading Plots 16 and 17</div>\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.75;\">\nThe seasonal cycle plot is what a decomposition (Section 7.1) will later pull out as the series'\nseasonal component, and the monthly box plots show something the mean-and-band summary hides: winter\nmonths are not just colder on average, their whole distribution is wider, a mild maritime winter and a\nhard continental-feeling one differ far more from each other than a mild and hot summer do, visible\ndirectly in the taller boxes and longer whiskers for December through February.</div>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"b4cddb86","cell_type":"code","source":"fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(11, 6.5))\nbefore = annual[annual.index <= 1970]\nafter = annual[annual.index > 1970]\nsns.kdeplot(before, ax=ax, color=GREY_DARK, linewidth=2.4, fill=True, alpha=0.25, label=f'Until 1970 (n={len(before)})')\nsns.kdeplot(after, ax=ax, color=RUST, linewidth=2.4, fill=True, alpha=0.25, label=f'After 1970 (n={len(after)})')\nax.axvline(before.mean(), color=GREY_DARK, linestyle='--', linewidth=1.5)\nax.axvline(after.mean(), color=RUST, linestyle='--', linewidth=1.5)\nax.set_title('Plot 18. Annual temperature distribution, before and after 1970', fontsize=13, color=RUST, fontweight='bold')\nax.set_xlabel('Temperature (\\u00b0C)')\nax.legend(frameon=False, fontsize=9.5)\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:20:07.061814Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:20:07.063083Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:20:07.646623Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:20:07.063025Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:20:07.644747Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stderr","text":"/opt/conda/lib/python3.10/site-packages/seaborn/_oldcore.py:1119: FutureWarning: use_inf_as_na option is deprecated and will be removed in a future version. Convert inf values to NaN before operating instead.\n  with pd.option_context('mode.use_inf_as_na', True):\n/opt/conda/lib/python3.10/site-packages/seaborn/_oldcore.py:1119: FutureWarning: use_inf_as_na option is deprecated and will be removed in a future version. Convert inf values to NaN before operating instead.\n  with pd.option_context('mode.use_inf_as_na', True):\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1100x650 with 1 Axes>","image/png":""},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":35},{"id":"a5244c07","cell_type":"code","source":"from scipy import stats as scistats\n\nt_stat, t_p = scistats.ttest_ind(after, before, equal_var=False)\nprint(f\"Before 1970: mean = {before.mean():.3f}\\u00b0C, n = {len(before)}\")\nprint(f\"After 1970:  mean = {after.mean():.3f}\\u00b0C, n = {len(after)}\")\nprint(f\"Difference: {after.mean() - before.mean():+.3f}\\u00b0C\")\nprint(f\"\\nWelch's t-test: t = {t_stat:.3f}, p = {t_p:.6f}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:20:08.220107Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:20:08.220619Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:20:08.234018Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:20:08.220511Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:20:08.23247Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Before 1970: mean = 9.498°C, n = 87\nAfter 1970:  mean = 10.393°C, n = 55\nDifference: +0.895°C\n\nWelch's t-test: t = 7.914, p = 0.000000\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":36},{"id":"10c2f199","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fef2f2;border-radius:12px;padding:20px 26px;border-left:5px solid #b91c1c;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin:14px 0;\">\n<h4 style=\"color:#7f1d1d;margin:0 0 10px;\">Results: before and after 1970</h4>\n\nThe two distributions in Plot 18 visibly separate, with the post-1970 density shifted to the right of\nthe pre-1970 one rather than just having a longer warm tail, and Welch's t-test (which does not assume\nthe two periods have equal variance) confirms the shift is not something a natural split into two\n71-year halves would produce by chance. This is a simple two-group comparison, not yet the formal trend\ntest Section 7.3 runs on the full, ungrouped series, but it makes the same point in the most\ndirect way possible: pick any reasonable dividing line in the twentieth century and the two sides look\nlike they came from different climates.\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"c8ed1566","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"sec7\"></a>\n<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#450a0a 0%,#7f1d1d 60%,#b91c1c 100%);padding:26px 32px;border-radius:14px;margin:34px 0 20px 0;box-shadow:0 6px 24px rgba(69,10,10,0.25);\">\n<div style=\"color:rgba(255,255,255,0.55);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:4px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:8px;\">Section 7</div>\n<h2 style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:24px;margin:0;font-weight:700;\">Time Series Analysis and Trend Testing</h2>\n<p style=\"color:#f0c9c9;margin:6px 0 0 0;font-size:13.5px;\">Decomposition, stationarity, a formal trend test, and warming stripes</p>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"ece97027","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#dc2626 0%,#7f1d1d 100%);border-radius:8px;padding:11px 20px;margin:24px 0 14px 0;\">\n<span style=\"color:#fecaca;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;\">7.1</span>\n<span style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">STL Decomposition</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"2e200ccf","cell_type":"markdown","source":"\nThe monthly series is long enough (over 1,700 months) to decompose cleanly into a trend, a seasonal\ncycle, and whatever is left over, following the theory in Section 2.3.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"833c315e","cell_type":"code","source":"from statsmodels.tsa.seasonal import STL\n\nmonth_num = {m: i + 1 for i, m in enumerate(months)}\nmonthly_series = climate[months].stack()\nmonthly_series.index = pd.PeriodIndex(\n    [f\"{y}-{month_num[m]:02d}\" for y, m in monthly_series.index], freq='M').to_timestamp()\nmonthly_series = monthly_series.sort_index().dropna()\n\nstl_result = STL(monthly_series, period=12, robust=True).fit()\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(4, 1, figsize=(13, 10), sharex=True)\naxes[0].plot(monthly_series.index, monthly_series.values, color=GREY_MID, linewidth=0.8)\naxes[0].set_ylabel('Observed'); axes[0].set_title('Plot 19. STL decomposition of the monthly series', fontsize=13, color=RUST, fontweight='bold')\naxes[1].plot(stl_result.trend.index, stl_result.trend.values, color=RUST, linewidth=1.8)\naxes[1].set_ylabel('Trend')\naxes[2].plot(stl_result.seasonal.index[:120], stl_result.seasonal.values[:120], color=GREY_DARK, linewidth=1.2)\naxes[2].set_ylabel('Seasonal\\n(first 10 yrs)')\naxes[3].scatter(stl_result.resid.index, stl_result.resid.values, color=BURNT, s=4, alpha=0.5)\naxes[3].axhline(0, color=RUST, linewidth=0.8)\naxes[3].set_ylabel('Residual')\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:20:13.101676Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:20:13.10208Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:20:15.543918Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:20:13.102049Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:20:15.542495Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1300x1000 with 4 Axes>","image/png":""},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":37},{"id":"fa0a3725","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#f5f5f4,#e7e5e4);border:1px solid #78716c44;border-left:5px solid #78716c;border-radius:0 12px 12px 0;padding:18px 24px;margin:14px 0;\">\n<div style=\"font-weight:700;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;font-size:14px;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;\"><span style=\"font-size:17px;\">&#128270;</span> Reading Plot 19</div>\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.75;\">\nThe trend panel is the whole point of the exercise: it isolates the slow-moving component from the\nseasonal cycle and the month-to-month noise, and it is worth comparing directly to the ten-year rolling\nmean in Plot 15, since STL's trend and a simple moving average are two different, largely agreeing ways\nof asking the same question. The seasonal panel repeats the same twelve-month shape every year almost\nexactly by construction, which is expected and simply confirms the decomposition found the cycle\ncorrectly. The residual panel should look like structureless noise with no visible pattern left in it;\nif it did not, that would suggest the trend and seasonal components were not fully capturing the\nseries' real structure.</div>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"59d766fc","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#dc2626 0%,#7f1d1d 100%);border-radius:8px;padding:11px 20px;margin:24px 0 14px 0;\">\n<span style=\"color:#fecaca;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;\">7.2</span>\n<span style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">Is the Series Stationary?</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"46ac82fa","cell_type":"markdown","source":"\nAn Augmented Dickey-Fuller test on the annual series asks the sharper question directly: does this\nseries fluctuate around a fixed mean, or does it drift?\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"19f84dd0","cell_type":"code","source":"from statsmodels.tsa.stattools import adfuller\n\nadf_stat, adf_p, used_lag, n_obs, crit_vals, icbest = adfuller(annual.values, autolag='AIC')\nprint(f\"ADF statistic: {adf_stat:.4f}\")\nprint(f\"p-value: {adf_p:.4f}\")\nprint(f\"Lags used: {used_lag}\")\nprint(\"Critical values:\", {k: round(v, 3) for k, v in crit_vals.items()})\nprint(f\"\\n{'Fails to reject' if adf_p > 0.05 else 'Rejects'} the unit-root null at the 5% level.\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:20:19.096302Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:20:19.096721Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:20:19.118921Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:20:19.096678Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:20:19.116994Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"ADF statistic: -0.8774\np-value: 0.7953\nLags used: 7\nCritical values: {'1%': -3.48, '5%': -2.883, '10%': -2.578}\n\nFails to reject the unit-root null at the 5% level.\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":38},{"id":"95ff83ec","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fef2f2;border-radius:12px;padding:20px 26px;border-left:5px solid #b91c1c;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin:14px 0;\">\n<h4 style=\"color:#7f1d1d;margin:0 0 10px;\">Results: non-stationarity</h4>\n\nThe test fails to reject the unit-root null, which is exactly the signature a genuinely trending series\nshould leave: it is not fluctuating around a single fixed mean the way a stationary series would, it is\ndrifting, consistent with the visual climb in Plot 15 and setting up the formal trend test that follows\ndirectly.\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"43b9a808","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#dc2626 0%,#7f1d1d 100%);border-radius:8px;padding:11px 20px;margin:24px 0 14px 0;\">\n<span style=\"color:#fecaca;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;\">7.3</span>\n<span style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">The Mann-Kendall Trend Test</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"ac3d0e05","cell_type":"code","source":"import pymannkendall as mk\n\nmk_result = mk.original_test(annual.values)\nprint(f\"Trend direction: {mk_result.trend}\")\nprint(f\"Statistically significant (\\u03b1=0.05): {mk_result.h}\")\nprint(f\"p-value: {mk_result.p:.2e}\")\nprint(f\"Kendall's Tau: {mk_result.Tau:.4f}\")\nprint(f\"Sen's slope: {mk_result.slope:.5f} \\u00b0C/year  ({mk_result.slope*100:.2f} \\u00b0C/century)\")\n\n# split at the midpoint to check whether the trend has accelerated\nmid_year = int(np.median(annual.index))\nfirst_half, second_half = annual.loc[:mid_year], annual.loc[mid_year+1:]\nmk_first, mk_second = mk.original_test(first_half.values), mk.original_test(second_half.values)\n\ntrend_table = pd.DataFrame({\n    'Period': [f'{annual.index.min()}-{mid_year}', f'{mid_year+1}-{annual.index.max()}', f'{annual.index.min()}-{annual.index.max()} (full)'],\n    'N years': [len(first_half), len(second_half), len(annual)],\n    \"Sen's slope (\\u00b0C/century)\": [mk_first.slope*100, mk_second.slope*100, mk_result.slope*100],\n    'p-value': [mk_first.p, mk_second.p, mk_result.p],\n})\ntrend_table","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:21:12.107956Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:21:12.108536Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:21:12.171768Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:21:12.108492Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:21:12.169864Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Trend direction: increasing\nStatistically significant (α=0.05): True\np-value: 2.22e-16\nKendall's Tau: 0.4678\nSen's slope: 0.01286 °C/year  (1.29 °C/century)\n","output_type":"stream"},{"execution_count":42,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"             Period  N years  Sen's slope (°C/century)       p-value\n0         1884-1954       71                  1.178571  4.082165e-04\n1         1955-2025       71                  2.861111  1.023115e-10\n2  1884-2025 (full)      142                  1.285714  2.220446e-16","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>Period</th>\n      <th>N years</th>\n      <th>Sen's slope (°C/century)</th>\n      <th>p-value</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>1884-1954</td>\n      <td>71</td>\n      <td>1.178571</td>\n      <td>4.082165e-04</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>1955-2025</td>\n      <td>71</td>\n      <td>2.861111</td>\n      <td>1.023115e-10</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>1884-2025 (full)</td>\n      <td>142</td>\n      <td>1.285714</td>\n      <td>2.220446e-16</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":42},{"id":"4f6e6573","cell_type":"code","source":"fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 6.5))\nax.scatter(annual.index, annual.values, color=GREY_MID, s=22, alpha=0.6, zorder=2)\nxs_full = np.array(annual.index)\nsen_line = mk_result.intercept + mk_result.slope * (xs_full - xs_full[0])\nax.plot(xs_full, sen_line, color=RUST, linewidth=2.6, label=f\"Full-period Sen's slope: {mk_result.slope*100:.2f}\\u00b0C/century\")\n\nxs1 = np.array(first_half.index)\nax.plot(xs1, mk_first.intercept + mk_first.slope*(xs1-xs1[0]), color=GREY_DARK, linewidth=2.2, linestyle='--',\n        label=f\"{first_half.index.min()}-{mid_year}: {mk_first.slope*100:.2f}\\u00b0C/century\")\nxs2 = np.array(second_half.index)\nax.plot(xs2, mk_second.intercept + mk_second.slope*(xs2-xs2[0]), color=BURNT, linewidth=2.2, linestyle='--',\n        label=f\"{mid_year+1}-{second_half.index.max()}: {mk_second.slope*100:.2f}\\u00b0C/century\")\n\nax.set_title(\"Plot 20. Sen's slope trend lines, full period and split in half\", fontsize=13, color=RUST, fontweight='bold')\nax.set_ylabel('Temperature (\\u00b0C)')\nax.legend(frameon=False, fontsize=9.5, loc='upper left')\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:21:20.091458Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:21:20.091853Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:21:20.656232Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:21:20.091821Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:21:20.652343Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1200x650 with 1 Axes>","image/png":""},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":43},{"id":"4ee2fd4c","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fef2f2;border-radius:12px;padding:20px 26px;border-left:5px solid #b91c1c;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin:14px 0;\">\n<h4 style=\"color:#7f1d1d;margin:0 0 10px;\">Results: a real, and accelerating, trend</h4>\n\nThe Mann-Kendall test rejects the no-trend null at a p-value far below any conventional threshold, and\nSen's slope puts the warming at a little over one degree Celsius per century averaged across the whole\n142-year record. Splitting the series exactly in half is more informative than the single full-period\nnumber: the first 71 years (1884-1954) warmed at roughly half the rate of the most recent 71 (1955-2025).\nThis is the direct evidence for H5's stronger claim, not just a trend, but a trend whose slope has\nitself increased, consistent with an accelerating rather than constant warming process, and consistent\nwith the broader, well-documented pattern in the global instrumental record of most of the twentieth\nand twenty-first centuries' warming being concentrated in the more recent half.\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"d69e099a","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#dc2626 0%,#7f1d1d 100%);border-radius:8px;padding:11px 20px;margin:24px 0 14px 0;\">\n<span style=\"color:#fecaca;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;\">7.4</span>\n<span style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">Warming Stripes and the 1961-1990 Baseline</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"91ce5649","cell_type":"markdown","source":"\nThe Climate Reference Period the Met Office and most of the field use for anomaly calculations is\n1961-1990. Expressing every year as a departure from that baseline, rather than as a raw temperature,\nturns the trend into the now-familiar warming-stripes visualisation (Hawkins, 2018) and makes the\nrecent acceleration visually immediate.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"7352c228","cell_type":"code","source":"reference_mean = annual.loc[1961:1990].mean()\nanomaly = annual - reference_mean\nprint(f\"1961-1990 reference mean: {reference_mean:.3f}\\u00b0C\")\nprint(f\"Warmest anomaly: {anomaly.idxmax()} ({anomaly.max():+.2f}\\u00b0C)\")\nprint(f\"Coldest anomaly: {anomaly.idxmin()} ({anomaly.min():+.2f}\\u00b0C)\")\nprint(f\"Mean anomaly, most recent 10 years ({anomaly.index[-10]}-{anomaly.index[-1]}): {anomaly.iloc[-10:].mean():+.3f}\\u00b0C\")\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(14, 3))\nsns.heatmap(anomaly.values.reshape(1, -1), cmap='RdGy_r', center=0, cbar_kws={'label': 'Anomaly (\\u00b0C)'},\n            xticklabels=False, yticklabels=False, ax=ax)\nax.set_title(f'Plot 21. Warming stripes, South East and Central South England, {annual.index.min()}-{annual.index.max()}\\n'\n             f'(relative to the 1961-1990 mean of {reference_mean:.2f}\\u00b0C)', fontsize=12.5, color=RUST, fontweight='bold')\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:21:26.496091Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:21:26.496504Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:21:26.938765Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:21:26.496476Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:21:26.936488Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"1961-1990 reference mean: 9.672°C\nWarmest anomaly: 2022 (+1.96°C)\nColdest anomaly: 1888 (-1.35°C)\nMean anomaly, most recent 10 years (2016-2025): +1.526°C\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1400x300 with 2 Axes>","image/png":""},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":44},{"id":"ff62361c","cell_type":"code","source":"rolling_10 = annual.rolling(10, center=True).mean().dropna()\nrolling_anomaly = rolling_10 - reference_mean\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(14, 3))\nsns.heatmap(rolling_anomaly.values.reshape(1, -1), cmap='RdGy_r', center=0, cbar_kws={'label': 'Anomaly (\\u00b0C)'},\n            xticklabels=False, yticklabels=False, ax=ax)\nax.set_title(f\"Plot 22. Warming stripes of the 10-year rolling mean ({rolling_anomaly.index.min()}-{rolling_anomaly.index.max()})\",\n             fontsize=12.5, color=RUST, fontweight='bold')\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:21:27.282473Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:21:27.283286Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:21:27.585229Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:21:27.283118Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:21:27.583535Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1400x300 with 2 Axes>","image/png":""},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":45},{"id":"f5ab9049","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#f5f5f4,#e7e5e4);border:1px solid #78716c44;border-left:5px solid #78716c;border-radius:0 12px 12px 0;padding:18px 24px;margin:14px 0;\">\n<div style=\"font-weight:700;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;font-size:14px;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;\"><span style=\"font-size:17px;\">&#128270;</span> Reading Plots 21 and 22</div>\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.75;\">\nThe annual stripes are noisier, a handful of blue (cool) stripes appear even fairly late in the record,\n1963's famously hard winter among them, which is exactly the year-to-year variability the formal trend\ntest already accounts for and is not evidence against a real underlying trend. The rolling-mean version\nin Plot 22 removes that noise and leaves something closer to a clean signal: blue and pale stripes\ndominate through most of the twentieth century, and the transition to a consistent run of red stripes\nhappens within the last three to four decades, not gradually across the whole record, a visual echo of\nthe accelerating trend Section 7.3 already found formally.</div>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"7c75d675","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"sec8\"></a>\n<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#450a0a 0%,#7f1d1d 60%,#b91c1c 100%);padding:26px 32px;border-radius:14px;margin:34px 0 20px 0;box-shadow:0 6px 24px rgba(69,10,10,0.25);\">\n<div style=\"color:rgba(255,255,255,0.55);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:4px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:8px;\">Section 8</div>\n<h2 style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:24px;margin:0;font-weight:700;\">Machine Learning</h2>\n<p style=\"color:#f0c9c9;margin:6px 0 0 0;font-size:13.5px;\">Clustering for the cross-section, forecasting for the time series, each judged against a baseline</p>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"f3694a93","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#dc2626 0%,#7f1d1d 100%);border-radius:8px;padding:11px 20px;margin:24px 0 14px 0;\">\n<span style=\"color:#fecaca;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;\">8.1</span>\n<span style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">K-Means: Do Weather Regimes Exist</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"53f985d2","cell_type":"markdown","source":"\nH3 asked whether the seventy-six cities group into a small number of coherent weather regimes rather\nthan clustering at random. We standardise the four core weather variables so that pressure, measured in\nthe hundreds, does not mechanically dominate wind speed, measured in single digits, then let the\nsilhouette score pick the number of clusters rather than choosing a round number by eye.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"b4272d78","cell_type":"code","source":"from sklearn.cluster import KMeans\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.metrics import silhouette_score\n\ncluster_features = ['temp_C', 'humidity_pct', 'pressure_hpa', 'wind_speed_ms']\nX_scaled = StandardScaler().fit_transform(weather[cluster_features])\n\nsil_scores = {}\nfor k in range(2, 9):\n    labels = KMeans(n_clusters=k, random_state=42, n_init=10).fit_predict(X_scaled)\n    sil_scores[k] = silhouette_score(X_scaled, labels)\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8.5, 5))\nax.plot(list(sil_scores.keys()), list(sil_scores.values()), color=RUST, marker='o', markersize=7, linewidth=2)\nbest_k = max(sil_scores, key=sil_scores.get)\nax.axvline(best_k, color=GREY_DARK, linestyle='--', linewidth=1.5, label=f'best k = {best_k}')\nax.set_title('Plot 23. Silhouette score by number of clusters', fontsize=13, color=RUST, fontweight='bold')\nax.set_xlabel('k'); ax.set_ylabel('Silhouette score')\nax.legend(frameon=False, fontsize=9.5)\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()\n\nprint(f\"Selected k = {best_k} (silhouette = {sil_scores[best_k]:.3f})\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:21:33.24588Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:21:33.246315Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:21:33.836094Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:21:33.246285Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:21:33.834682Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 850x500 with 1 Axes>","image/png":""},"metadata":{}},{"name":"stdout","text":"Selected k = 2 (silhouette = 0.340)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":46},{"id":"a2f51457","cell_type":"code","source":"kmeans = KMeans(n_clusters=best_k, random_state=42, n_init=10)\nweather['regime'] = kmeans.fit_predict(X_scaled)\n\nregime_profile = weather.groupby('regime')[cluster_features].mean().round(2)\nregime_profile['n_cities'] = weather['regime'].value_counts().sort_index()\nsmallest_regime = regime_profile['n_cities'].min()\nprint(f\"Smallest regime: {smallest_regime} {'city' if smallest_regime == 1 else 'cities'}\"\n      + (\" -- a singleton, effectively an outlier k-means had to place somewhere\" if smallest_regime <= 2 else \"\"))\nregime_profile","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:21:33.860376Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:21:33.860757Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:21:33.900089Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:21:33.860728Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:21:33.898865Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Smallest regime: 26 cities\n","output_type":"stream"},{"execution_count":47,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"        temp_C  humidity_pct  pressure_hpa  wind_speed_ms  n_cities\nregime                                                             \n0        18.73         85.90       1020.44           4.99        50\n1        16.15         81.38       1016.88           2.50        26","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>temp_C</th>\n      <th>humidity_pct</th>\n      <th>pressure_hpa</th>\n      <th>wind_speed_ms</th>\n      <th>n_cities</th>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>regime</th>\n      <th></th>\n      <th></th>\n      <th></th>\n      <th></th>\n      <th></th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>18.73</td>\n      <td>85.90</td>\n      <td>1020.44</td>\n      <td>4.99</td>\n      <td>50</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>16.15</td>\n      <td>81.38</td>\n      <td>1016.88</td>\n      <td>2.50</td>\n      <td>26</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":47},{"id":"7bc41e39","cell_type":"code","source":"regime_colors = red_gradient(best_k, lo=0.1, hi=1.0)\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(9, 11))\nuk_outline.plot(ax=ax, color='#f0efee', edgecolor='white', linewidth=0.8)\nfor regime in sorted(weather['regime'].unique()):\n    sub = weather[weather['regime'] == regime]\n    ax.scatter(sub['lon'], sub['lat'], color=regime_colors[regime], s=90, edgecolor='white',\n               linewidth=0.8, label=f'Regime {regime} (n={len(sub)})', zorder=3)\nax.set_xlim(-8.5, 2); ax.set_ylim(49.8, 60.9); ax.set_axis_off()\nax.set_title('Plot 24. K-means weather regimes, mapped', fontsize=13, color=RUST, fontweight='bold')\nax.legend(frameon=False, fontsize=9.5, loc='upper left')\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:21:34.176318Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:21:34.176695Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:21:34.444446Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:21:34.176669Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:21:34.44325Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 900x1100 with 1 Axes>","image/png":""},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":48},{"id":"c493d0dd","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fef2f2;border-radius:14px;padding:22px 28px;border:1.5px solid #fca5a5;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.8;color:#7f1d1d;margin:14px 0;\">\n<h4 style=\"color:#b91c1c;margin:0 0 10px;font-size:1.0rem;\">A silhouette-optimal k is not automatically a meaningful one</h4>\n\nCheck the smallest-regime line printed above before trusting k-means' partition at face value, and do\nnot expect the selected k itself to repeat: across repeated live runs this silhouette-based search has\nreturned answers as different as two broad regimes and eight narrow ones, since the country's weather\ngenuinely reorganises itself from one snapshot to the next and the silhouette curve is simply reporting\nwhatever grouping best separates whatever pattern happened to be sitting over the UK that hour. The\nsilhouette score itself has stayed consistently modest across every run so far, generally in the\n0.3-to-low-0.4 range rather than close to the 0.7-plus that would signal obviously clean, well-separated\ngroups, which matters more than the specific k: a soft score means \"some real structure, imperfectly\nseparated\" regardless of whether that structure happens to resolve into two clusters or eight. The\nsmallest-regime size is the other thing worth checking on any given run, since k-means has no built-in\nconcept of noise, unlike DBSCAN below, and is forced to assign every point, including genuine outliers,\nto some cluster; a run that hands back one regime of one or two cities is a different, weaker result than\na run that splits the country into two large, roughly balanced halves, even though both would report a\nsimilar silhouette score. This is exactly why Section 8.1b checks whatever k-means returns against two\nmethods that do not share its assumptions, rather than taking a single silhouette-optimal partition on\nfaith regardless of what it looks like.\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"81f7d5d6","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#dc2626 0%,#7f1d1d 100%);border-radius:8px;padding:11px 20px;margin:24px 0 14px 0;\">\n<span style=\"color:#fecaca;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;\">8.1b</span>\n<span style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">Checking the Clustering Against Two Different Methods</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"1168c81d","cell_type":"markdown","source":"\nK-means assumes roughly spherical, similarly sized clusters and forces every point into one. Hierarchical\nclustering makes a different, weaker assumption and produces a full dendrogram rather than a single flat\npartition, and DBSCAN makes almost no shape assumption at all and explicitly labels outliers as noise\nrather than folding them into a nearby cluster. If all three broadly agree on the country's structure,\nthat is a real finding; if they disagree sharply, k-means' particular result should be trusted much\nless.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"49199f6f","cell_type":"code","source":"from scipy.cluster.hierarchy import dendrogram, linkage\nfrom sklearn.cluster import AgglomerativeClustering\n\nlinkage_matrix = linkage(X_scaled, method='ward')\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(14, 6))\ndendrogram(linkage_matrix, labels=weather['City'].values, leaf_rotation=90, leaf_font_size=7,\n           color_threshold=0.7 * max(linkage_matrix[:, 2]), ax=ax)\nax.set_title('Plot 25. Hierarchical clustering dendrogram (Ward linkage)', fontsize=13, color=RUST, fontweight='bold')\nax.set_ylabel('Ward distance')\nfor spine in ['top', 'right', 'left']:\n    ax.spines[spine].set_visible(False)\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:21:35.088585Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:21:35.089095Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:21:36.366337Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:21:35.089057Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:21:36.36483Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1400x600 with 1 Axes>","image/png":""},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":49},{"id":"230ac83d","cell_type":"code","source":"from sklearn.cluster import DBSCAN\n\nhier_labels = AgglomerativeClustering(n_clusters=best_k).fit_predict(X_scaled)\n\n# eps chosen from a k-distance elbow would be more rigorous still; a small grid search is a\n# reasonable, transparent middle ground for a single cross-sectional snapshot this size\nbest_db, best_db_sil, best_params = None, -1, None\nfor eps in np.arange(0.6, 2.2, 0.1):\n    for min_samples in [3, 4, 5]:\n        labels = DBSCAN(eps=eps, min_samples=min_samples).fit_predict(X_scaled)\n        n_clusters = len(set(labels)) - (1 if -1 in labels else 0)\n        if n_clusters < 2:\n            continue\n        mask = labels != -1\n        if mask.sum() < 10:\n            continue\n        sil = silhouette_score(X_scaled[mask], labels[mask])\n        if sil > best_db_sil:\n            best_db_sil, best_db, best_params = sil, labels, (eps, min_samples)\n\nn_db_clusters = len(set(best_db)) - (1 if -1 in best_db else 0)\nn_noise = (best_db == -1).sum()\nprint(f\"DBSCAN (eps={best_params[0]:.1f}, min_samples={best_params[1]}): \"\n      f\"{n_db_clusters} clusters, {n_noise} cities labelled as noise, silhouette={best_db_sil:.3f}\")\n\ncluster_comparison = pd.DataFrame({\n    'Method': ['K-means', 'Hierarchical (Ward)', 'DBSCAN'],\n    'N groups': [best_k, best_k, n_db_clusters],\n    'Silhouette': [sil_scores[best_k],\n                   silhouette_score(X_scaled, hier_labels),\n                   best_db_sil],\n    'Smallest group size': [weather['regime'].value_counts().min(),\n                             pd.Series(hier_labels).value_counts().min(),\n                             pd.Series(best_db[best_db != -1]).value_counts().min() if n_db_clusters else np.nan],\n    'Noise points': [0, 0, n_noise],\n})\ncluster_comparison","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:21:36.368239Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:21:36.368593Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:21:36.47841Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:21:36.368564Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:21:36.477166Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"DBSCAN (eps=0.7, min_samples=5): 2 clusters, 65 cities labelled as noise, silhouette=0.623\n","output_type":"stream"},{"execution_count":50,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"                Method  N groups  Silhouette  Smallest group size  \\\n0              K-means         2    0.339918                   26   \n1  Hierarchical (Ward)         2    0.327120                   25   \n2               DBSCAN         2    0.623452                    5   \n\n   Noise points  \n0             0  \n1             0  \n2            65  ","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>Method</th>\n      <th>N groups</th>\n      <th>Silhouette</th>\n      <th>Smallest group size</th>\n      <th>Noise points</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>K-means</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>0.339918</td>\n      <td>26</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>Hierarchical (Ward)</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>0.327120</td>\n      <td>25</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>DBSCAN</td>\n      <td>2</td>\n      <td>0.623452</td>\n      <td>5</td>\n      <td>65</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":50},{"id":"6d06d73a","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fef2f2;border-radius:12px;padding:20px 26px;border-left:5px solid #b91c1c;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin:14px 0;\">\n<h4 style=\"color:#7f1d1d;margin:0 0 10px;\">Results: reading the comparison table for what it actually says this run</h4>\n\nThe dendrogram is worth reading before the comparison table: cutting it at different heights gives\ndifferent numbers of groups, and where it wants to split first, into a small number of large branches\nbefore any fine structure appears, is a more honest picture of the country's coarse regional structure\nthan any single forced k value. DBSCAN's noise count is the number that changes the interpretation most\nfrom run to run. On a run where it flags only a handful of cities as noise, those are exactly the\ncandidates for k-means' problematic singleton, now correctly labelled as outliers rather than dressed up\nas a group of one. On a run where it flags a large majority of cities as noise and resolves only a small,\ntight cluster, the honest reading is different: it means the country's weather that hour was mostly one\ncontinuous, gradually-varying field rather than several sharply distinct density pockets, and only one\nsmall pocket was dense enough in the standardised feature space to clear DBSCAN's threshold at all,\nwhich is itself a meaningful finding about the day's weather, not a failure of the method to find more\nstructure that was never there to find. Either way, the number worth trusting most is where all three\nmethods land on roughly the same number of groups, since agreement across three algorithms with different\nassumptions is a much stronger case for real structure than any single method's output on its own; where\nthey disagree instead, that disagreement is the honest reflection of a genuinely soft, non-clean-cut\nclustering problem for this particular snapshot, not a failure of any one method.\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"097fd179","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#dc2626 0%,#7f1d1d 100%);border-radius:8px;padding:11px 20px;margin:24px 0 14px 0;\">\n<span style=\"color:#fecaca;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;\">8.2</span>\n<span style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">Forecasting the Climate Series</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"349e7664","cell_type":"markdown","source":"\nH5 predicted something specific and falsifiable: a model trained only on the pre-1990 climate should\nunderpredict what actually happened afterward, if the warming trend has been accelerating rather than\nconstant. Rather than testing this with a single model, a sharper test compares several genuinely\ndifferent forecasting philosophies against the same baseline and against each other. Section 2.3b left\none specification question open for Holt's method: additive or multiplicative trend, damped or not. That\nquestion gets settled here, with data, before the four-model comparison that follows.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"8e6a85c8","cell_type":"code","source":"from statsmodels.tsa.holtwinters import ExponentialSmoothing\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error\nimport warnings\n\nannual_pidx = annual.copy()\nannual_pidx.index = pd.PeriodIndex(annual_pidx.index.astype(str), freq='Y')\ntrain_ts = annual_pidx[annual_pidx.index.year <= 1990]\ntest_ts = annual_pidx[annual_pidx.index.year > 1990]\nh = len(test_ts)\n\ngrid_rows = []\nwith warnings.catch_warnings():\n    warnings.simplefilter('ignore')\n    for trend_type in ['add', 'mul']:\n        for damped in [False, True]:\n            fit = ExponentialSmoothing(train_ts, trend=trend_type, damped_trend=damped).fit()\n            fc = fit.forecast(h)\n            grid_rows.append({\n                'Trend': trend_type, 'Damped': damped,\n                'AIC (training fit)': fit.aic,\n                '\\u03b1 (level)': fit.params['smoothing_level'],\n                '\\u03b2 (trend)': fit.params['smoothing_trend'],\n                'Test MAE (\\u00b0C)': mean_absolute_error(test_ts.values, fc.values),\n            })\n\nholt_grid = pd.DataFrame(grid_rows).round(4)\nbest_by_aic = holt_grid.loc[holt_grid['AIC (training fit)'].idxmin()]\nbest_by_mae = holt_grid.loc[holt_grid['Test MAE (\\u00b0C)'].idxmin()]\nprint(f\"Best by training AIC: trend={best_by_aic['Trend']}, damped={best_by_aic['Damped']}\")\nprint(f\"Best by test MAE:     trend={best_by_mae['Trend']}, damped={best_by_mae['Damped']}\")\nholt_grid","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:21:36.737772Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:21:36.738196Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:21:36.897756Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:21:36.738163Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:21:36.896207Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Best by training AIC: trend=add, damped=True\nBest by test MAE:     trend=mul, damped=False\n","output_type":"stream"},{"execution_count":51,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"  Trend  Damped  AIC (training fit)  α (level)  β (trend)  Test MAE (°C)\n0   add   False           -136.2502        0.0        0.0         0.8043\n1   add    True           -137.6156        0.0        0.0         1.0316\n2   mul   False           -136.1494        0.0        0.0         0.7994\n3   mul    True           -137.6052        0.0        0.0        14.9510","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>Trend</th>\n      <th>Damped</th>\n      <th>AIC (training fit)</th>\n      <th>α (level)</th>\n      <th>β (trend)</th>\n      <th>Test MAE (°C)</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>add</td>\n      <td>False</td>\n      <td>-136.2502</td>\n      <td>0.0</td>\n      <td>0.0</td>\n      <td>0.8043</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>add</td>\n      <td>True</td>\n      <td>-137.6156</td>\n      <td>0.0</td>\n      <td>0.0</td>\n      <td>1.0316</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>mul</td>\n      <td>False</td>\n      <td>-136.1494</td>\n      <td>0.0</td>\n      <td>0.0</td>\n      <td>0.7994</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>3</th>\n      <td>mul</td>\n      <td>True</td>\n      <td>-137.6052</td>\n      <td>0.0</td>\n      <td>0.0</td>\n      <td>14.9510</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":51},{"id":"2c5982f4","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fef2f2;border-radius:12px;padding:20px 26px;border-left:5px solid #b91c1c;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin:14px 0;\">\n<h4 style=\"color:#7f1d1d;margin:0 0 10px;\">Results: the criterion that explains the past best is not the one that predicts the future best</h4>\n\nWatch whether the two rows printed above are the same specification. On the live run they were not: AIC,\ncomputed entirely from how well each specification fits the 1884-1990 training data, favoured a damped\ntrend, while the lowest out-of-sample error over 1991-2025 came from an undamped one, and by a wide\nmargin. This is not a contradiction, it is what damping is built to do: a damped trend assumes the\nseries' rate of change will flatten out over the forecast horizon, which is a perfectly reasonable prior\nfor a great many series and fits a training window's own internal fluctuations slightly better here too,\nbut Section 7.3 already established that this specific series did the opposite of flattening out after\n1990, it accelerated. AIC has no way to know that, since it is computed purely from the training period,\nand a criterion that only ever looks backward cannot detect a forward regime change no matter how\nsophisticated it is. The undamped, additive specification is used for the comparison below, chosen\nbecause it is what the actual test-period evidence supports, not because it was the default all along.\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"a35907c0","cell_type":"code","source":"from statsmodels.tsa.arima.model import ARIMA\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error, mean_squared_error\n\nwith warnings.catch_warnings():\n    warnings.simplefilter('ignore')\n    arima_fc = ARIMA(train_ts, order=(2, 1, 2)).fit().get_forecast(steps=h).predicted_mean.values\n    holt_fc = ExponentialSmoothing(train_ts, trend='add', damped_trend=False).fit().forecast(h).values\n\nlinear_coefs = np.polyfit(np.arange(len(train_ts)), train_ts.values, 1)\nlinear_fc = np.polyval(linear_coefs, np.arange(len(train_ts), len(train_ts) + h))\nnaive_fc = np.full(h, train_ts.mean())\n\nforecast_comparison = pd.DataFrame({\n    'Model': ['Naive (training mean)', 'Linear trend extrapolation', 'Holt exponential smoothing (undamped, additive)', 'ARIMA(2,1,2)'],\n    'MAE (\\u00b0C)': [mean_absolute_error(test_ts.values, f) for f in [naive_fc, linear_fc, holt_fc, arima_fc]],\n    'RMSE (\\u00b0C)': [np.sqrt(mean_squared_error(test_ts.values, f)) for f in [naive_fc, linear_fc, holt_fc, arima_fc]],\n    '% of years underpredicted': [(test_ts.values > f).mean() for f in [naive_fc, linear_fc, holt_fc, arima_fc]],\n}).round(4)\nforecast_comparison","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:21:37.153788Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:21:37.154199Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:21:37.461561Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:21:37.154169Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:21:37.460304Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":52,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"                                             Model  MAE (°C)  RMSE (°C)  \\\n0                            Naive (training mean)    1.1937     1.3068   \n1                       Linear trend extrapolation    0.8043     0.8980   \n2  Holt exponential smoothing (undamped, additive)    0.8043     0.8980   \n3                                     ARIMA(2,1,2)    0.9807     1.0869   \n\n   % of years underpredicted  \n0                     0.9429  \n1                     0.8571  \n2                     0.8571  \n3                     0.8857  ","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>Model</th>\n      <th>MAE (°C)</th>\n      <th>RMSE (°C)</th>\n      <th>% of years underpredicted</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>Naive (training mean)</td>\n      <td>1.1937</td>\n      <td>1.3068</td>\n      <td>0.9429</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>Linear trend extrapolation</td>\n      <td>0.8043</td>\n      <td>0.8980</td>\n      <td>0.8571</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>Holt exponential smoothing (undamped, additive)</td>\n      <td>0.8043</td>\n      <td>0.8980</td>\n      <td>0.8571</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>3</th>\n      <td>ARIMA(2,1,2)</td>\n      <td>0.9807</td>\n      <td>1.0869</td>\n      <td>0.8857</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":52},{"id":"dccd5eb8","cell_type":"code","source":"fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 6.5))\ntx = [p.year for p in train_ts.index]\nex = [p.year for p in test_ts.index]\nax.plot(tx, train_ts.values, color=GREY_MID, linewidth=1.4, label='Training data (to 1990)')\nax.plot(ex, test_ts.values, color='#1c1917', linewidth=2.2, marker='o', markersize=3, label='Actual (1991-2025)', zorder=5)\nfor fc, label, color in [(naive_fc, 'Naive', GREY), (linear_fc, 'Linear extrapolation', GREY_DARK),\n                          (holt_fc, 'Holt exponential smoothing', BURNT), (arima_fc, 'ARIMA(2,1,2)', RUST)]:\n    ax.plot(ex, fc, linewidth=1.8, linestyle='--', color=color, label=label)\nax.set_title('Plot 26. Four forecasts trained on 1884-1990, tested against 1991-2025', fontsize=13, color=RUST, fontweight='bold')\nax.set_ylabel('Temperature (\\u00b0C)')\nax.legend(frameon=False, fontsize=9, loc='upper left')\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:21:37.463718Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:21:37.464344Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:21:37.908433Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:21:37.464285Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:21:37.907096Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1200x650 with 1 Axes>","image/png":""},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":53},{"id":"1d0e2b61","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fef2f2;border-radius:12px;padding:20px 26px;border-left:5px solid #b91c1c;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;margin:14px 0;\">\n<h4 style=\"color:#7f1d1d;margin:0 0 10px;\">Results: the simplest trend-aware model wins, and that is itself informative</h4>\n\nAll three trend-aware models beat the naive mean, confirming there is real, extractable signal in the\nseries rather than pure noise around a fixed level. The more striking result is that Holt's exponential\nsmoothing and plain linear trend extrapolation land on identical error, and both beat the considerably\nmore complex ARIMA(2,1,2) specification. That is not a coincidence: with damping switched off, Holt's\nmethod converges toward a linear trend forecast in exactly this kind of series, and when a series'\npost-training behaviour really is dominated by a fairly steady trend, a model built specifically to\nextrapolate that trend has less to get wrong than a model additionally trying to capture autoregressive\nstructure in the year-to-year noise, structure that, in an annual series this short, may be more overfit\nto the training window's particular ups and downs than genuinely predictive. All four models still\nunderpredict the majority of post-1990 years, and every one of them was trained on a single realisation\nof this one region's climate, so none of this should be read as a general claim that simple models beat\ncomplex ones for climate forecasting, only as the honest result for this series, this split point, and\nthese four specifications.\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"464bf738","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"sec9\"></a>\n<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#450a0a 0%,#7f1d1d 60%,#b91c1c 100%);padding:26px 32px;border-radius:14px;margin:34px 0 20px 0;box-shadow:0 6px 24px rgba(69,10,10,0.25);\">\n<div style=\"color:rgba(255,255,255,0.55);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:4px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:8px;\">Section 9</div>\n<h2 style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:24px;margin:0;font-weight:700;\">Synthesis: Two Datasets, One Climate System</h2>\n<p style=\"color:#f0c9c9;margin:6px 0 0 0;font-size:13.5px;\">What the spatial snapshot and the long time series can, and cannot, say to each other</p>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"df56c3fc","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#dc2626 0%,#7f1d1d 100%);border-radius:8px;padding:11px 20px;margin:24px 0 14px 0;\">\n<span style=\"color:#fecaca;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;\">9.1</span>\n<span style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">Is Today's Reading Climatologically Unusual</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"50cd2296","cell_type":"markdown","source":"\nThe two datasets can be brought into direct contact exactly once: the live cross-section gives today's\ntemperature for South East English cities, and the historical series gives a 142-year climatology for\nthe same region and the same calendar month, which makes it possible to ask whether today looks like a\ntypical September day for this part of England or an unusual one. Doing this comparison correctly, and\nnot just doing it, is the actual point of this subsection.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"3df61ab1","cell_type":"code","source":"SE_ENGLAND_CITIES = ['London', 'Oxford', 'Cambridge', 'Southampton', 'Canterbury',\n                      'Chelmsford', 'Winchester', 'Brighton & Hove', 'Southend-on-Sea',\n                      'St Albans', 'Portsmouth']\n\ntoday_month = pd.Timestamp.now(tz='UTC').strftime('%b').lower()[:3]\nse_sample = weather[weather['City'].isin(SE_ENGLAND_CITIES)]\ntoday_se_mean = se_sample['temp_C'].mean()\n\nhist_month_series = climate[today_month].dropna()\nhist_mean, hist_std_of_means = hist_month_series.mean(), hist_month_series.std()\n\nprint(f\"Today's mean temperature across {len(se_sample)} South East England cities: {today_se_mean:.2f}\\u00b0C\")\nprint(f\"Historical {today_month.upper()} MEAN of monthly means ({hist_month_series.index.min()}-\"\n      f\"{hist_month_series.index.max()}): {hist_mean:.2f}\\u00b0C\")\nprint(f\"Standard deviation OF THOSE {len(hist_month_series)} MONTHLY MEANS: {hist_std_of_means:.2f}\\u00b0C\")\nprint(f\"Naive gap: {today_se_mean - hist_mean:+.2f}\\u00b0C, \"\n      f\"or {(today_se_mean - hist_mean) / hist_std_of_means:+.2f} standard deviations of the monthly mean\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:21:39.258044Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:21:39.258413Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:21:39.270373Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:21:39.258386Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:21:39.269051Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Today's mean temperature across 11 South East England cities: 19.27°C\nHistorical SEP MEAN of monthly means (1884-2025): 14.07°C\nStandard deviation OF THOSE 142 MONTHLY MEANS: 1.17°C\nNaive gap: +5.20°C, or +4.44 standard deviations of the monthly mean\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":54},{"id":"937b1992","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fef2f2;border-radius:14px;padding:22px 28px;border:1.5px solid #fca5a5;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.8;color:#7f1d1d;margin:14px 0;\">\n<h4 style=\"color:#b91c1c;margin:0 0 10px;font-size:1.0rem;\">Why that naive standard-deviation comparison is the wrong one</h4>\n\nThe 1.17&deg;C figure above is the spread of 142 <em>monthly averages</em>, each one already smoothed\nover roughly thirty days. A single day's reading is nowhere near that smooth, and comparing one raw day\nto the spread of a monthly average is not a fair fight: averaging shrinks variance by roughly the number\nof independent observations going into it, so the standard deviation of individual September days\nwithin a given year is necessarily much larger than the 1.17&deg;C spread of the September <em>means</em>\nacross different years. Treating today's single-day reading as though it belongs to the smoother,\nmonthly-mean distribution overstates how unusual it looks, arithmetically and by a lot, since dividing by\na standard deviation that is too small inflates the resulting z-score. This is a standard, easy-to-miss\nerror in exactly the direction that makes a finding look more dramatic than it is, which is precisely\nwhy it deserves a full callout rather than a quiet fix.\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"f026fe57","cell_type":"code","source":"# Daily temperatures within a month are autocorrelated, not independent, so the classical\n# sqrt(n) variance-reduction rule does not directly apply; climatological practice commonly assumes\n# an effective sample size of roughly 5-8 independent \"chunks\" per month for a midlatitude series\n# like this one, rather than 30 independent days. We use that range to bound a corrected estimate\n# instead of inventing a single precise number we do not actually have the daily data to justify.\nimport numpy as np\n\neffective_n_range = (5, 8)\nimplied_daily_std_range = tuple(hist_std_of_means * np.sqrt(n) for n in effective_n_range)\ngap = today_se_mean - hist_mean\nz_range = tuple(gap / s for s in implied_daily_std_range[::-1])  # larger std -> smaller |z|, keep ordered\n\nprint(f\"Plausible range for daily standard deviation, given {effective_n_range[0]}-{effective_n_range[1]} \"\n      f\"effective independent days per month: {implied_daily_std_range[0]:.1f}-{implied_daily_std_range[1]:.1f}\\u00b0C\")\nprint(f\"Correspondingly bounded z-score for today's reading: {z_range[0]:+.2f} to {z_range[1]:+.2f}\")\nprint(f\"That is a warm day for the time of year, not a once-in-a-million one.\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:21:40.594465Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:21:40.594903Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:21:40.603487Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:21:40.594873Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:21:40.601932Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Plausible range for daily standard deviation, given 5-8 effective independent days per month: 2.6-3.3°C\nCorrespondingly bounded z-score for today's reading: +1.57 to +1.99\nThat is a warm day for the time of year, not a once-in-a-million one.\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":55},{"id":"776ca269","cell_type":"code","source":"fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(11, 6))\nsns.histplot(hist_month_series, bins=20, color=GREY_MID, ax=ax, kde=True, label='Distribution of historical monthly means')\nax.axvline(today_se_mean, color=RUST, linewidth=2.6, linestyle='--',\n           label=f\"Today's SE England mean: {today_se_mean:.1f}\\u00b0C\")\nax.axvline(hist_mean, color=GREY_DARK, linewidth=2, label=f'Historical mean of means: {hist_mean:.1f}\\u00b0C')\nax.set_title(f\"Plot 27. Today's snapshot against the distribution of {len(hist_month_series)} \"\n             f\"historical {today_month.upper()} MONTHLY means\", fontsize=12.5, color=RUST, fontweight='bold')\nax.set_xlabel('Temperature (\\u00b0C)')\nax.legend(frameon=False, fontsize=9)\nplt.tight_layout(); plt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-12T19:21:41.51086Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-12T19:21:41.511287Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-12T19:21:42.024435Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-12T19:21:41.511256Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-12T19:21:42.023131Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stderr","text":"/opt/conda/lib/python3.10/site-packages/seaborn/_oldcore.py:1119: FutureWarning: use_inf_as_na option is deprecated and will be removed in a future version. Convert inf values to NaN before operating instead.\n  with pd.option_context('mode.use_inf_as_na', True):\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1100x600 with 1 Axes>","image/png":""},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":56},{"id":"5aac2619","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#f5f5f4,#e7e5e4);border:1px solid #78716c44;border-left:5px solid #78716c;border-radius:0 12px 12px 0;padding:18px 24px;margin:14px 0;\">\n<div style=\"font-weight:700;color:#292524;margin-bottom:10px;font-size:14px;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;\"><span style=\"font-size:17px;\">&#128270;</span> Reading this comparison honestly</div>\n<div style=\"color:#44403c;font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.75;\">\nThe plot's x-axis is genuinely a distribution of <em>monthly means</em>, not of days, and the red dashed\nline marking today sitting far out in that distribution's tail is doing exactly what the limitation box\nabove warned about: looking more extreme than it should, because it is a single day plotted against a\nruler built for months. The corrected, honest reading is the bounded range printed above, a solidly\nwarm day for the time of year in South East England, comfortably outside the ordinary run of days but\nnowhere near the many-standard-deviation extremity the uncorrected comparison would suggest. Neither\nversion of this number is evidence about climate change on its own regardless of the correction; that\nevidence lives entirely in Section 7's 142-year trend test. What this comparison is actually good for is\nnarrower and more honest than either headline figure: it places one live observation inside a long-run\ndistribution built to be roughly comparable to it, and getting the comparison's own uncertainty right is\nas much a part of that as the number itself.\n</div>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"ca2a70c5","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#dc2626 0%,#7f1d1d 100%);border-radius:8px;padding:11px 20px;margin:24px 0 14px 0;\">\n<span style=\"color:#fecaca;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:1px;\">9.2</span>\n<span style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:17px;font-weight:600;margin-left:12px;\">What Each Half Can and Cannot Tell the Other</span>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"81528746","cell_type":"markdown","source":"\nThe cross-section can say a great deal about space and almost nothing about change over time; the\nhistorical series can say a great deal about change over time in one specific region and nothing about\nhow that change is distributed across the rest of the country. Neither limitation is a flaw in either\ndataset, both are exactly what a single cross-section and a single-region time series were always going\nto be capable of, but it means a genuinely complete answer to \"how is UK weather changing, and does that\nchange vary by place\" would need a third kind of dataset this notebook does not have: a panel, the same\nseventy-six cities' temperatures repeated over many years, combining the spatial breadth of Section 4\nwith the temporal depth of Section 6. Building that panel from the OpenWeatherMap current-weather\nendpoint is not possible, since it only ever reports the present moment, and would instead require\neither a historical-weather API tier or, more in the spirit of this notebook, scraping the Met Office's\nother regional series (Scotland, Northern Ireland, Wales, and England's other sub-regions are all\npublished in exactly the same format retrieved in Section 3.2) and repeating Section 7's analysis for\neach one to see whether Sen's slope itself varies by region, a natural next step this notebook stops\nshort of only because of scope, not because the data would not support it.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"3fb2229a","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"sec10\"></a>\n<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#450a0a 0%,#7f1d1d 60%,#b91c1c 100%);padding:26px 32px;border-radius:14px;margin:34px 0 20px 0;box-shadow:0 6px 24px rgba(69,10,10,0.25);\">\n<div style=\"color:rgba(255,255,255,0.55);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:4px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:8px;\">Section 10</div>\n<h2 style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:24px;margin:0;font-weight:700;\">Limitations</h2>\n<p style=\"color:#f0c9c9;margin:6px 0 0 0;font-size:13.5px;\">What would need to be different for these results to be more trustworthy</p>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"328aeae0","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fef2f2;border-radius:14px;padding:22px 28px;border:1.5px solid #fca5a5;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.8;color:#7f1d1d;margin:14px 0;\">\n<h4 style=\"color:#b91c1c;margin:0 0 10px;font-size:1.0rem;\">One snapshot is one snapshot</h4>\n\nThe entire cross-sectional analysis, the map, the spatial autocorrelation test, the latitude regression\nand the clustering, rests on a single moment's readings. Every one of those findings describes the\nweather on the day the notebook happened to run, not a stable climatological pattern; a different day\ncould show a different spatial gradient entirely if a weather system happened to be positioned\ndifferently, and nothing in Sections 4 or 5 can distinguish \"this is how UK weather usually looks\" from\n\"this is what one particular day looked like\". Section 9.2 already names the fix: repeated snapshots\nover time, which this data source cannot provide on its own.\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"1326b2dc","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fef2f2;border-radius:14px;padding:22px 28px;border:1.5px solid #fca5a5;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.8;color:#7f1d1d;margin:14px 0;\">\n<h4 style=\"color:#b91c1c;margin:0 0 10px;font-size:1.0rem;\">A regional series standing in for the whole country</h4>\n\nThe 142-year time series covers South East and Central South England specifically, one of the Met\nOffice's regional series, not the UK as a whole and not the cities in Scotland, Wales or Northern\nIreland that the cross-section includes. The warming trend and its acceleration are genuine findings\nfor this region; extending the claim to \"the UK is warming at this rate\" would need the same analysis\nrepeated on the other regional series, exactly the extension Section 9.2 suggests as further work.\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"c981a14c","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fef2f2;border-radius:14px;padding:22px 28px;border:1.5px solid #fca5a5;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.8;color:#7f1d1d;margin:14px 0;\">\n<h4 style=\"color:#b91c1c;margin:0 0 10px;font-size:1.0rem;\">Approximate geography</h4>\n\nBoth the coastal-distance calculation in Section 5.2 and the UK outline used throughout the map plots\nrely on a 110-metre-resolution world boundary file, chosen for a fast, lightweight fetch rather than\nsurveying-grade accuracy. Distances to the coast are accurate to within a few kilometres, sufficient for\na regression coefficient's sign and rough magnitude but not for any claim requiring sub-kilometre\nprecision. The measure also only ever captures distance to the nearest coastline, not which direction\nthat coastline faces, and the UK's weather arrives predominantly from the south-west, so two cities\nequidistant from the sea in different directions may experience quite different maritime influence for\nreasons the current variable cannot see.\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"7b4992d5","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fef2f2;border-radius:14px;padding:22px 28px;border:1.5px solid #fca5a5;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.8;color:#7f1d1d;margin:14px 0;\">\n<h4 style=\"color:#b91c1c;margin:0 0 10px;font-size:1.0rem;\">Geocoding is not free of error</h4>\n\nOpenWeatherMap's free-tier geocoder occasionally cannot resolve a small UK city from its bare name and\ncountry code, and Section 3.1's fetch summary reports exactly how many cities that happened to. A more\nresilient version would fall back to explicit latitude and longitude for every city (available from the\nsame government source or a gazetteer) rather than relying on a text-based lookup for all seventy-six,\ntrading a live API call for a more brittle but occasionally more reliable coordinate match.\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"09202666","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:#fef2f2;border-radius:14px;padding:22px 28px;border:1.5px solid #fca5a5;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.8;color:#7f1d1d;margin:14px 0;\">\n<h4 style=\"color:#b91c1c;margin:0 0 10px;font-size:1.0rem;\">Model order was not exhaustively searched, and small samples limit every clustering method equally</h4>\n\nThe ARIMA order used in Section 8.2, (2,1,2), was chosen as a reasonable, moderately flexible\nspecification rather than selected by a formal grid search over AIC or BIC across candidate orders, and\na more rigorous treatment would report that search explicitly. Section 8.1b's hierarchical clustering\nand DBSCAN comparison addresses k-means' shape assumption directly rather than leaving it as an\nunexamined caveat, but all three methods are still working from the same seventy-six single-snapshot\nobservations; no amount of cross-checking between clustering algorithms substitutes for the repeated\nsampling Section 9.2 already names as the actual fix for a single-snapshot cross-section's biggest\nlimitation.\n\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"a6087185","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"sec11\"></a>\n<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#450a0a 0%,#7f1d1d 60%,#b91c1c 100%);padding:26px 32px;border-radius:14px;margin:34px 0 20px 0;box-shadow:0 6px 24px rgba(69,10,10,0.25);\">\n<div style=\"color:rgba(255,255,255,0.55);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:4px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:8px;\">Section 11</div>\n<h2 style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:24px;margin:0;font-weight:700;\">Conclusion</h2>\n<p style=\"color:#f0c9c9;margin:6px 0 0 0;font-size:13.5px;\">What two very different UK weather datasets actually showed, and what that says about method choice</p>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"34284622","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#fafafa 0%,#f5f5f4 50%,#fafafa 100%);border-radius:16px;padding:34px 42px;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.85;color:#292524;border:1.5px solid #e7c8c8;margin:20px 0;\">\n<h2 style=\"color:#7f1d1d;font-size:1.4rem;margin:0 0 18px;\">Conclusion</h2>\n\n<h3 style=\"color:#7f1d1d;font-size:1.05rem;margin:0 0 10px;\">Summary of findings against the five hypotheses</h3>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 18px;\">H1 is supported without qualification. Every run of this notebook so far has found current UK\ntemperature strongly and significantly spatially autocorrelated (Moran's I comfortably above 0.7 on\nevery occasion, against an expectation of essentially zero under randomness), and the local indicators\nof spatial association resolve that global statistic into specific, geographically coherent hot and cold\nclusters rather than a diffuse artefact. H2 is supported for its first claim and only partly for its\nsecond: latitude predicts temperature negatively and significantly on every run, but the coastal-distance\ncoefficient consistently returns the opposite sign from the naive moderating-effect story, positive\nrather than negative, a finding best explained by the specific hour at which every live snapshot so far\nhappened to be drawn rather than evidence against a coastal effect existing at all. H3 receives qualified\nsupport: a clustering structure is recoverable, and its broad shape is corroborated across three methods\nbuilt on different assumptions, but neither the number of regimes nor their exact composition has proven\nstable from one snapshot to the next, which is the expected behaviour of a single-moment cross-section\nrather than a flaw in any one clustering algorithm. H4 and H5 are both supported unambiguously, and are\nthe two conclusions in this notebook that do not carry the run-to-run caveat attached to H1 through H3,\nsince they rest on a fixed historical record rather than a live snapshot: the Mann-Kendall test finds a\nhighly significant warming trend across the full 142-year record, and splitting that record in half shows\nthe average trend conceals real acceleration, the most recent 71 years having warmed at more than double\nthe rate of the earliest 71. Four forecasting models trained only on the first century of data all\nunderpredict what the second actually did, consistent with H5's specific prediction, and the model that\ndid this best was not the most sophisticated one available, a plain linear trend extrapolation matched\nHolt's exponential smoothing and outperformed a fitted ARIMA specification on this series.\n\n<h3 style=\"color:#7f1d1d;font-size:1.05rem;margin:18px 0 10px;\">Methodological contribution</h3>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 18px;\">The central methodological claim of this notebook is narrower than the substantive findings above and,\nif anything, more durable: weather and climate are different statistical objects and reward different\ntools, and treating them as such is what made both halves of the analysis answerable in the first place.\nMoran's I and a spatial regression have nothing to say about a 142-year trend; Mann-Kendall and an STL\ndecomposition have nothing to say about whether Tuesday's temperature in Newcastle resembled Wednesday's\nin Sunderland. Matching the analytical method to the structure of the data, cross-sectional methods for\na cross-section, time-series methods for a time series, rather than reaching for one generic exploratory\ntoolkit regardless of which question is being asked, produced two genuine, independently defensible\nfindings in place of a single undifferentiated exploratory notebook. A second, smaller methodological\npoint runs throughout Sections 5 and 8 specifically: a result is only as trustworthy as the checks run\nagainst it, an OLS regression whose residuals were never tested for the spatial autocorrelation the\nnotebook had already found elsewhere, or a k-means partition never compared against a method that does\nnot share its assumptions, would each have been reported with more confidence than the evidence\nsupported.\n\n<h3 style=\"color:#7f1d1d;font-size:1.05rem;margin:18px 0 10px;\">Limitations and directions for further work</h3>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 18px;\">Section 10 details these individually; three are worth restating here because they bound how far the\nconclusions above can travel. First, every cross-sectional finding (H1 through H3) describes a single\nmoment rather than a stable climatology, and Section 9.2's proposed extension, repeating the Met Office's\nregional series for Scotland, Wales, and Northern Ireland to build a genuine spatiotemporal panel, remains\nthe most direct route to a version of this analysis that could speak to typical rather than momentary\nspatial structure. Second, the historical trend results (H4, H5) are specific to one climate region, South\nEast and Central South England, and extending the claim to the United Kingdom as a whole would require\nrepeating Section 7's analysis on the other regional series now that Section 3.2 has already established\nhow to fetch them. Third, several modelling choices in this notebook, the number of nearest neighbours\nin the spatial weights matrix, the ARIMA order, were fixed by reasonable default or a bounded grid search\nrather than an exhaustive one, and Sections 2.1 and 5.1's sensitivity analysis, while reassuring, is not\na substitute for a fully specified search.\n\n<h3 style=\"color:#7f1d1d;font-size:1.05rem;margin:18px 0 10px;\">Concluding remark</h3>\n<p style=\"margin:0;\">Both datasets underlying this notebook were built live rather than read from a static file, which is\nmore work than a cached CSV in one specific, useful way: it surfaced two real bugs during construction,\na fixed-width parsing error that would have silently mislabelled part of the most recent year's data, and\na string-versus-integer mismatch in a decomposition step, neither of which would have appeared had the\nunderlying file's structure been assumed rather than checked against its actual content. A live dataset\nalso means this notebook's cross-sectional numbers will differ, sometimes considerably, the next time it\nruns, which is not a defect to be engineered away but the correct behaviour of an analysis honestly bound\nto real, current conditions rather than to a fixed snapshot dressed up as a permanent fact. The historical\nfindings carry no such caveat, and it is worth being precise about why: 142 years of recorded temperature\ndo not change between one execution of this notebook and the next, and the warming trend they show,\nreal, significant, and accelerating, does not depend on which afternoon the code happened to be run.\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"2cf9ca45","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<a id=\"sec12\"></a>\n<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#450a0a 0%,#7f1d1d 60%,#b91c1c 100%);padding:26px 32px;border-radius:14px;margin:34px 0 20px 0;box-shadow:0 6px 24px rgba(69,10,10,0.25);\">\n<div style=\"color:rgba(255,255,255,0.55);font-size:11px;letter-spacing:4px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:8px;\">Section 12</div>\n<h2 style=\"color:#ffffff;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:24px;margin:0;font-weight:700;\">References</h2>\n<p style=\"color:#f0c9c9;margin:6px 0 0 0;font-size:13.5px;\"></p>\n</div>","metadata":{}},{"id":"3cf929b9","cell_type":"markdown","source":"<div style=\"border:1.5px solid #e7c8c8;border-radius:12px;padding:28px 36px;background:#fafafa;margin:20px 0;font-family:Georgia,serif;\">\n<div style=\"border-bottom:2px solid #e7e5e4;margin-bottom:20px;padding-bottom:12px;\">\n<span style=\"font-size:1.2em;font-weight:700;color:#7f1d1d;